var e=[{id:`js-closure`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`What is a closure? Give a real use.`,text:`A closure is a function that keeps access to the variables of the scope it was created in, even after that scope has finished running. Real uses: debounce/throttle (closing over a timer id), private state in factory functions, and memoisation caches. Stale closures in React are the same mechanism going wrong: an effect or callback captured an old render's values. Fix with correct dependencies, the updater form of setState, or a ref.`,hook:`🎒 A function packs a backpack of the variables around its birthplace and carries it wherever it goes.`,tags:[`scope`,`functions`]},{id:`js-event-loop`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`Explain the event loop.`,text:`JavaScript runs on one thread with a call stack. Async callbacks wait in queues. When the stack is empty, all microtasks run (Promise .then, queueMicrotask), then one macrotask (setTimeout, I/O, events), then the browser may render, and the loop repeats. A microtask that keeps queueing microtasks starves rendering and freezes the page, exactly like an infinite loop.`,hook:`🎢 The VIP line (microtasks) is always emptied before one person from the regular line (macrotasks) gets on the ride.`,tags:[`async`,`event-loop`]},{id:`js-hoisting`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`What is hoisting?`,text:`Declarations are registered before code runs. var is hoisted and initialised to undefined; function declarations are hoisted whole. let, const and class are hoisted too but sit in the temporal dead zone, so touching them before their line throws.`,hook:`📋 The engine reads the guest list first: var guests get a blank name tag, let/const guests wait outside until their line is called.`,tags:[`scope`]},{id:`js-var-let-const`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:"`var` vs `let` vs `const`?",text:`var is function-scoped and leaks out of blocks like loops. let and const are block-scoped. const stops you reassigning the binding, but an object or array behind it can still be mutated.`,hook:`🔒 const locks the label, not the box: you can't stick the label on a new box, but you can rearrange what's inside.`,tags:[`scope`]},{id:`js-this`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:"How does `this` work? What do `call`, `apply` and `bind` do?",text:`In a regular function, this depends on how it's called: obj.fn() gives obj, a plain call gives undefined (strict mode), new gives the new object. Arrow functions don't have their own this; they take it from where they're defined. call/apply invoke with an explicit this (args listed vs as an array); bind returns a new function with this fixed. This is why arrow functions are the default for callbacks and event handlers: no .bind(this) needed.`,hook:`👉 Regular functions ask "who called me?"; arrow functions ask "where was I born?".`,tags:[`functions`,`this`]},{id:`js-prototypes`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`Prototypal inheritance in one breath?`,text:`Every object has a hidden link to a prototype object. When a property isn't found on the object, the lookup walks up that chain until it finds it or reaches null. class is syntax on top of this mechanism.`,hook:`🪜 Can't find it on your shelf? Climb the ladder to your parent's shelf, then theirs, until you hit the ceiling (null).`,tags:[`objects`,`prototypes`]},{id:`js-equality`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:"`==` vs `===`, and `null` vs `undefined`?",text:`=== compares type and value with no conversion, so use it by default. == coerces first, which produces surprises like [] == false being true. undefined means "never assigned"; null means "deliberately empty". Use ?? to default only on null/undefined and || to default on any falsy value.`,hook:`🧪 == is a chemist who transforms both sides before comparing; === refuses to touch the samples.`,tags:[`types`,`coercion`]},{id:`js-promise-combinators`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:"Promises: `all` vs `allSettled` vs `race` vs `any`?",text:`all resolves with every value but rejects on the first rejection. allSettled waits for every promise and reports each outcome. race settles with whichever settles first, success or failure. any resolves with the first success and rejects only if all fail. Use allSettled when one failure mustn't sink the rest, such as loading independent dashboard widgets.`,hook:`🏃 all = the whole team must finish; allSettled = report card for everyone; race = first across the line; any = first one who actually wins.`,tags:[`async`,`promises`]},{id:`js-async-await`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:"Promises vs `async`/`await`, and how do you handle errors?",text:`A Promise is a value that will exist later; you chain .then()/.catch(). async/await is syntax over Promises that reads top to bottom, with try/catch for errors. A promise you don't await can reject outside the try. Run independent calls in parallel with Promise.all instead of awaiting them one by one.`,hook:`📦 await is "pause here until the parcel arrives", and try/catch is signing for it in case it arrives broken.`,tags:[`async`,`promises`,`errors`]},{id:`js-event-delegation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`Event bubbling, capturing and delegation?`,text:`An event travels down to the target (capture phase), then back up through its ancestors (bubble phase). Delegation puts one listener on a parent and checks event.target, which is ideal for long or dynamic lists. stopPropagation stops the journey; preventDefault cancels the browser's default action.`,hook:`🫧 Events are bubbles rising from where you clicked: put one net at the top instead of one on every bubble.`,tags:[`dom`,`events`]},{id:`js-copying`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`Shallow copy vs deep copy?`,text:`Spread and Object.assign copy only one level, so nested objects are still shared. For a deep copy use structuredClone(obj). JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)) drops dates, undefined, Maps and functions.`,hook:`📸 A shallow copy photocopies the cover page; the chapters inside are still the original book.`,tags:[`objects`]},{id:`js-debounce-throttle`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`Debounce vs throttle?`,text:`Debounce waits until calls stop for N ms, then fires once, which suits search-as-you-type. Throttle fires at most once every N ms while calls keep coming, which suits scroll and resize handlers.`,hook:`🛗 Debounce is a lift door that waits until people stop walking in; throttle is a turnstile that lets one through per second.`,tags:[`performance`,`timers`]},{id:`js-modules`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`ES modules vs CommonJS?`,text:`ES modules use import/export, are static (so bundlers can analyse and tree-shake them) and can load asynchronously. CommonJS uses require, is dynamic and synchronous. Bundlers can only reliably tree-shake ESM.`,hook:`🗺️ ESM hands the bundler a map before the trip; CommonJS decides the route while driving.`,tags:[`modules`,`tooling`]},{id:`js-array-methods`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:"`map`, `filter`, `reduce`, `forEach`: when do you use each?",text:`map transforms each item into a new array, filter keeps the items that match, reduce folds everything into one value, and forEach is for side effects only (it returns nothing). The first three leave the original array untouched, which is why they suit immutable React updates.`,hook:`🏭 map reshapes, filter sieves, reduce squashes, forEach just visits.`,tags:[`arrays`]},{id:`js-map-set-weakmap`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:"What are `Map`, `Set` and `WeakMap` for?",text:`Map takes keys of any type and keeps insertion order. Set stores unique values with fast membership checks, which makes deduping easy. WeakMap holds its object keys weakly, so it can cache per-object data without stopping garbage collection.`,hook:`🗂️ Map is a labelled filing cabinet, Set is a guest list with no duplicates, WeakMap is sticky notes that fall off when the object is thrown away.`,tags:[`data-structures`]},{id:`js-memory-leaks`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`What causes memory leaks in frontend code?`,text:`Memory that's no longer needed but still referenced, so the garbage collector can't reclaim it. Common causes: event listeners, intervals or subscriptions that are never cleaned up; detached DOM nodes still held in variables; and caches that grow without limit.`,hook:`🚰 A leak is a tap you opened in useEffect and never turned off in the cleanup.`,tags:[`performance`,`memory`]},{id:`js-destructuring-spread`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`javascript`,title:`Destructuring, spread and rest?`,text:`Destructuring pulls values out of objects or arrays into variables in one line. Spread (...) expands an array or object (copying, merging, passing arguments). Rest (...) does the opposite and collects remaining items into one array or object. Same syntax, opposite jobs.`,hook:`🎁 Spread unpacks the box onto the table; rest sweeps whatever's left back into a box.`,tags:[`syntax`]},{id:`br-url-to-pixels`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`What happens between typing a URL and seeing the page?`,text:`DNS lookup, then TCP + TLS handshake, then the HTTP request. HTML streams in and the parser builds the DOM incrementally while a preload scanner fetches CSS, JS and images it spots ahead. CSS becomes the CSSOM; DOM + CSSOM form the render tree; layout computes geometry; paint fills pixels into layers; composite assembles the layers on the GPU. Scripts can pause any of this.`,hook:`🏗️ Address → road → delivery → blueprint (DOM) → paint spec (CSSOM) → measure (layout) → paint → assemble (composite).`,tags:[`networking`,`pipeline`]},{id:`br-dom`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`What is the DOM?`,text:`The browser's in-memory tree of the document: a node for every element, text and comment, plus the API (querySelector, appendChild…) that JavaScript uses to read and change it. It's built incrementally as HTML arrives, which is why rendering can start before the download finishes.`,hook:`🌳 The DOM is the page's family tree that JavaScript can edit live.`,tags:[`dom`]},{id:`br-cssom`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`What is the CSSOM, and how is it different from the DOM?`,text:`The CSSOM is the tree of parsed style rules with the cascade resolved. The DOM says what exists; the CSSOM says how it looks. Unlike the DOM it can't be used half-built, because a later rule can override an earlier one, so the browser waits for all the CSS before rendering. That's why CSS is render-blocking.`,hook:`🎨 DOM is the furniture list; CSSOM is the interior designer's final spec, and nobody paints until the spec is signed off.`,tags:[`css`,`dom`]},{id:`br-render-tree`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`What is the render tree?`,text:`DOM + CSSOM combined, containing only what will be visible. It excludes <head, <script and anything with display: none, and it includes pseudo-elements like ::before. Elements with visibility: hidden stay in, because they still take up space.`,hook:`🎭 The render tree is the cast list for tonight's show: understudies (display: none) aren't on it, invisible actors (visibility: hidden) still hold their spot on stage.`,tags:[`pipeline`]},{id:`br-critical-path`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`What is the critical rendering path, and how do you optimise it?`,text:`The steps from HTML to first pixels: DOM, CSSOM, render tree, layout, paint. Optimise by cutting the number of critical resources (inline critical CSS, defer the rest), their size (minify, compress) and the round trips (preload key assets, preconnect to origins you'll need).`,hook:`🚦 Fewer, smaller, sooner: fewer blocking files, smaller files, fetched sooner.`,tags:[`performance`,`pipeline`]},{id:`br-css-blocking`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`Why is CSS render-blocking, and why can it block JavaScript too?`,text:`The browser won't paint until the CSSOM is complete, or you'd see a flash of unstyled content. And because a script might read computed styles (getComputedStyle, offsetWidth), the browser won't execute a script until the CSS before it has loaded. A slow stylesheet therefore delays scripts too. Keep CSS in the <head and keep it small.`,hook:`🧍 Scripts queue behind stylesheets because they might ask "what does this look like?", and only the CSS knows.`,tags:[`css`,`performance`]},{id:`br-script-loading`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:'Why does a `<script>` block parsing? What do `async`, `defer` and `type="module"` change?',text:`A classic script can call document.write or change the DOM, so the parser stops, downloads, runs it, then continues. async downloads in parallel and runs as soon as it arrives, in no fixed order (good for analytics). defer downloads in parallel and runs after parsing, in document order (good for app code). Module scripts are deferred by default.`,hook:`📬 async = open each letter the moment it lands; defer = stack them and read in order after the meeting.`,tags:[`javascript`,`performance`]},{id:`br-domcontentloaded-load`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:"`DOMContentLoaded` vs `load`?",text:`DOMContentLoaded fires when the HTML is parsed and deferred scripts have run, without waiting for images or iframes. load fires when everything, images included, has finished.`,hook:`🏠 DOMContentLoaded = the house is built; load = the furniture has been delivered too.`,tags:[`events`]},{id:`br-reflow`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`What is layout (reflow), and what triggers it?`,text:`Layout calculates every element's size and position. It's triggered by geometry changes (width, height, margin, top, font size, adding/removing elements, changing text) and by window resizes. It's expensive because one change can ripple to parents, children and siblings.`,hook:`🧱 Move one brick and the builder re-measures the whole wall.`,tags:[`performance`,`layout`]},{id:`br-reflow-repaint-composite`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`Reflow vs repaint vs composite: which CSS properties trigger which?`,text:`Changing width/top/margin costs layout + paint + composite. Changing color/background/box-shadow costs paint + composite. Changing transform/opacity costs composite only, which can run on the GPU off the main thread. That's why animations should use transform and opacity.`,hook:`💸 Geometry is expensive, colour is mid-price, transform/opacity are free rides on the GPU.`,tags:[`performance`,`animation`,`css`]},{id:`br-layout-thrashing`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`What is layout thrashing, and how do you avoid it?`,text:`Interleaving DOM writes and layout reads in a loop, e.g. setting a style then reading offsetHeight, again and again. Each read forces a synchronous layout. Fix it by batching all reads first and then all writes, or by doing writes inside requestAnimationFrame.`,hook:`🏓 Write–read–write–read is ping-pong with the layout engine; read everything, then write everything.`,tags:[`performance`,`layout`]},{id:`br-forced-layout`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`Which reads force a synchronous layout?`,text:`offsetWidth/Height/Top, clientWidth/Height, scrollTop/Height, getBoundingClientRect(), getComputedStyle() and innerText. They're cheap on their own; they only get costly right after a write that invalidated layout.`,hook:`📏 Anything that asks "how big / where is it?" makes the browser stop and measure.`,tags:[`performance`,`layout`]},{id:`br-compositing-layers`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:"What are compositing layers, and what does `will-change` do?",text:`The browser can paint some elements onto separate layers that the GPU moves independently, so animating them never touches layout or paint. will-change: transform (or a 3D transform) promotes an element ahead of time. Too many layers waste GPU memory, so promote only what actually animates, and only while it animates.`,hook:`🎞️ Layers are acetate sheets on an overhead projector: slide a sheet around without redrawing it.`,tags:[`performance`,`animation`,`gpu`]},{id:`br-raf`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:"`requestAnimationFrame` vs `setTimeout` for animation?",text:`requestAnimationFrame runs your callback right before the next paint, synced to the display's refresh rate, and pauses in background tabs. setTimeout fires on its own clock, drifts against frames and causes jank or wasted work. At 60fps you have about 16.7ms per frame for JS, style, layout and paint together.`,hook:`🎬 rAF is the director shouting "action" exactly when the camera rolls; setTimeout is an actor guessing.`,tags:[`animation`,`event-loop`]},{id:`br-render-event-loop`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`Where does rendering fit into the event loop?`,text:`One task, then all microtasks, then (if a frame is due) rAF callbacks, style, layout and paint. So a long task, or a microtask loop that never ends, blocks rendering and the page freezes. requestIdleCallback runs when a frame has time to spare.`,hook:`🚌 The paint bus only leaves the station when the current task and all its microtasks have got off.`,tags:[`event-loop`,`pipeline`]},{id:`br-long-tasks`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`What is a long task, and why does it matter?`,text:`Any main-thread task over 50ms. While it runs, the page can't respond to input, which hurts INP. Break the work up (yield with setTimeout or scheduler.yield()), move it to a Web Worker, or defer it with useTransition in React.`,hook:`🐘 A long task is an elephant in a one-lane road: nothing gets past until it moves.`,tags:[`performance`,`inp`]},{id:`br-batch-dom`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`How do you insert many DOM nodes efficiently?`,text:`Build them off-document and insert once, with a DocumentFragment (or one innerHTML for trusted markup), so you get one layout instead of one per node. For very long lists, virtualise. In React you rarely touch this, because the commit phase already batches DOM writes.`,hook:`🧺 Carry the groceries in one basket, not one apple per trip.`,tags:[`dom`,`performance`]},{id:`br-innerhtml-textcontent`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:"`innerHTML` vs `textContent` vs `innerText`?",text:`innerHTML parses HTML, so it's an XSS risk with user data. textContent reads or sets raw text: fast and safe. innerText respects CSS (hidden text is excluded), so reading it forces a layout.`,hook:`💉 innerHTML injects, textContent just types, innerText looks before it speaks.`,tags:[`dom`,`security`]},{id:`br-shadow-dom`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`What is the Shadow DOM?`,text:`An encapsulated DOM subtree attached to an element, with scoped styles that don't leak in or out. It's the basis of Web Components and of native controls like <video.`,hook:`🏰 A walled garden inside the page: its styles stay in, the page's styles stay out.`,tags:[`dom`,`web-components`]},{id:`br-fonts`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`FOUC, FOIT and how fonts affect rendering?`,text:`FOUC is a flash of unstyled content (CSS arriving late). FOIT is invisible text while a web font loads. font-display: swap shows a fallback immediately (with a risk of layout shift); optional avoids the shift. Preload key fonts, or use a tool like next/font that self-hosts them and matches the fallback's metrics.`,hook:`🔤 Swap shows words now and changes clothes later; optional won't change clothes in public.`,tags:[`fonts`,`cls`,`performance`]},{id:`br-resource-hints`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:"What do `preload`, `prefetch` and `preconnect` do?",text:`preload fetches something the current page needs soon, at high priority (hero image, key font). prefetch fetches something the next page might need, at low priority. preconnect opens the DNS/TCP/TLS connection to an origin early.`,hook:`🛒 Preload = grab it now, prefetch = put it in the trolley for later, preconnect = open the shop door in advance.`,tags:[`performance`,`networking`]},{id:`br-contain`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:"What do `contain` and `content-visibility: auto` do?",text:`contain tells the browser an element's layout and paint don't affect the rest of the page, which limits how far a reflow spreads. content-visibility: auto skips rendering off-screen sections until they near the viewport. It's a cheap win on long pages.`,hook:`📦 contain puts an element in a sealed box; content-visibility doesn't even unpack boxes you can't see yet.`,tags:[`performance`,`css`]},{id:`br-debug-rendering`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`browser-rendering`,title:`How do you debug rendering performance?`,text:`Chrome DevTools → Performance: record, then look for long tasks and purple (layout) or green (paint) blocks. The Rendering tab has paint flashing and layout-shift regions; the Layers panel shows compositing. For React, the Profiler shows which components rendered and why.`,hook:`🩺 Record, find the red flags (long tasks), check the colour: purple means layout, green means paint.`,tags:[`performance`,`tooling`]},{id:`react-props-vs-state`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Props vs state?`,text:`Props are passed in by the parent and are read-only for the child. State is data a component owns and updates itself (via useState/useReducer), and updating it triggers a re-render.`,hook:`🎁 Props are gifts you're handed; state is what's in your own pocket.`,tags:[`state`,`props`]},{id:`react-rules-of-hooks`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`What are the rules of hooks, and why do they exist?`,text:`Call hooks only at the top level of a component or custom hook: never inside loops, conditions or nested functions, and never from plain JS functions. React tracks hooks by call order between renders, not by name, so a conditional hook shifts the order and desyncs its internal state.`,hook:`📚 React reads your hooks like a numbered list; skip one line and every answer after it lands in the wrong box.`,tags:[`hooks`]},{id:`react-immutability`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Why must state be immutable?`,text:`React compares state by reference. If you mutate an object and set the same reference, React sees no change and skips the render. Always create new objects or arrays. The flip side is useful: returning the identical object from a reducer on a no-op means nothing downstream re-renders.`,hook:`🪞 React only notices a new mirror, not a smudge on the old one.`,tags:[`state`,`immutability`]},{id:`react-useeffect`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"`useEffect`: dependencies, cleanup and common mistakes?",text:`It runs after paint whenever a dependency changes; its cleanup runs before the next run and on unmount. Common mistakes: missing dependencies (stale values), objects or functions as dependencies (it runs every render), no cleanup (leaks, race conditions), and using an effect for derived state that should simply be computed during render.`,hook:`🧹 An effect is a guest: it arrives after the party starts (paint), and must clean up before the next one or when the house closes.`,tags:[`hooks`,`effects`]},{id:`react-fetch-race`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"How do you fetch data in `useEffect` without race conditions?",text:`Abort or ignore the old request in the cleanup, so a slow old response can't overwrite a newer one. Better still, use a data library (TanStack Query, SWR) or fetch in a Server Component.`,hook:`🏁 When the query changes, cancel the old runner before the new one starts, or the slow one finishes last and "wins".`,tags:[`effects`,`data-fetching`,`async`]},{id:`react-strictmode`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Why does StrictMode run effects twice in development?`,text:`It mounts, unmounts and remounts components to surface effects without proper cleanup. If running twice breaks something, the effect isn't idempotent. This only happens in development.`,hook:`🧪 StrictMode is a stress test: plug it in, pull it out, plug it in. Good effects don't flinch.`,tags:[`effects`,`debugging`]},{id:`react-controlled-uncontrolled`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Controlled vs uncontrolled inputs?`,text:`Controlled: React state is the source of truth, updated via onChange; use it when you validate or derive UI from the value. Uncontrolled: the DOM holds the value and you read it with a ref or FormData; simpler for submit-only forms and file inputs.`,hook:`🎮 Controlled = React holds the joystick; uncontrolled = the input drives itself and you check where it ended up.`,tags:[`forms`]},{id:`react-custom-hooks`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`What's a custom hook? Give an example.`,text:`A function starting with use that composes other hooks to share stateful logic, not state itself. Each component that calls it gets its own independent state.`,hook:`🧩 A custom hook is a recipe, not a shared pot: everyone cooks their own portion.`,tags:[`hooks`,`reuse`]},{id:`react-memoisation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"`memo`, `useMemo`, `useCallback`: when do you use them?",text:`memo skips re-rendering a component when its props are shallow-equal. useMemo caches a computed value; useCallback caches a function's identity. Use them when the Profiler shows a real cost, or when reference stability matters (an effect dependency, a prop to a memoised child), not by default. The React Compiler now does most of this automatically. Say "I don't reach for these by default; I reach for them when the Profiler shows a problem." Inline objects and arrow functions passed as props are what usually break memo.`,hook:`💾 Memoisation is insurance: worth it for expensive things, a waste of premiums on cheap ones.`,tags:[`performance`,`memoisation`,`hooks`]},{id:`react-context`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Context: when do you use it, and what's the catch?`,text:`Context suits low-frequency global values: theme, auth user, locale. The catch is that every consumer re-renders when the provider's value reference changes, whatever part it reads. Fix it by splitting contexts, memoising the value, or using a selector-based store for frequently changing data.`,hook:`📢 Context is a loudspeaker: every listener hears every announcement, even ones not meant for them.`,tags:[`context`,`state`,`performance`]},{id:`react-prop-drilling`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Prop drilling: what is it, and how do you avoid it?`,text:`Passing a prop through several components that don't use it, just to reach a deep child. Fix it with composition first (pass the deep child as children or an element prop), then Context, then a store for genuinely global data.`,hook:`🚰 Don't run a pipe through every room; hand the thing directly to the room that needs it.`,tags:[`props`,`composition`]},{id:`react-refs`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Refs: what are they for? How do you pass one to a child?`,text:`A ref is either a handle to a DOM node or a mutable value that persists across renders without causing one (timer ids, latest values, previous props). In React 19, ref is an ordinary prop on function components; before that you needed forwardRef.`,hook:`📌 A ref is a sticky note React never reads, so changing it never triggers a re-render.`,tags:[`refs`,`dom`]},{id:`react-error-boundaries`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`What do error boundaries catch, and what do they miss?`,text:`They catch errors thrown during rendering, in lifecycle methods and in constructors of the tree below them. They don't catch errors in event handlers (use try/catch), async code (a rejected promise in an effect), server-side rendering, or the boundary itself.`,hook:`🥅 An error boundary is a safety net under the render trapeze, not under the whole circus.`,tags:[`errors`]},{id:`react-portals`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`Portals: why use them?`,text:`createPortal renders children into a different DOM node (e.g. document.body), which lets modals and tooltips escape overflow: hidden and z-index stacking contexts. Events still bubble through the React tree, not the DOM tree.`,hook:`🌀 A portal teleports the DOM, but the family tree (context, events) stays the same.`,tags:[`dom`,`modals`]},{id:`react-code-splitting`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`How do you do code splitting in React?`,text:`Use React.lazy(() = import('./X')) inside a <Suspense fallback (or next/dynamic). Split by route first, then by heavy, rarely used components such as editors, charts and modals.`,hook:`🧳 Pack the essentials; ship the heavy suitcase only when someone opens that door.`,tags:[`performance`,`bundling`]},{id:`react-18-19`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`What's new in React 18 and 19?`,text:`18: concurrent rendering, automatic batching everywhere, useTransition/useDeferredValue, streaming SSR with Suspense. 19: Actions (useActionState, useFormStatus, useOptimistic), the use() hook, ref as a plain prop, stable Server Components, and the React Compiler, which memoises automatically.`,hook:`🧭 18 made React interruptible; 19 made forms, async and memoisation feel built-in.`,tags:[`react-19`,`concurrency`]},{id:`react-transitions`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"`useTransition` vs `useDeferredValue`?",text:`Both mark work as low priority so React can interrupt it for urgent input like typing. Use useTransition when you own the state update (wrap the setter, and get an isPending flag). Use useDeferredValue for a value you receive (from props or a store) when you can't wrap the update.`,hook:`🎚️ Transition = "I'll mark my own update as slow"; deferred value = "I'll lag behind this value I was given".`,tags:[`concurrency`,`performance`]},{id:`react-uselayouteffect`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:"`useLayoutEffect` vs `useEffect`?",text:`useLayoutEffect runs after the DOM is updated but before the browser paints, and blocks the paint. Use it only to measure or adjust layout without flicker (tooltip positioning, scroll restoration). Everything else belongs in useEffect.`,hook:`🎭 Layout effects fix the costume before the curtain rises; regular effects happen while the audience watches.`,tags:[`effects`,`layout`]},{id:`react-hoc-render-props`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react`,title:`HOCs vs render props vs hooks?`,text:`All three share logic between components. HOCs and render props wrap components, which leads to wrapper hell and unclear prop sources. Hooks replaced them for most cases. HOCs still show up for cross-cutting wrappers such as auth guards.`,hook:`🪆 HOCs and render props are nesting dolls; hooks let you just take the doll out.`,tags:[`patterns`,`reuse`]},{id:`ri-virtual-dom`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`Virtual DOM vs real DOM: is the virtual DOM "faster"?`,text:`Not by itself, because nothing beats a perfectly targeted manual DOM update. The virtual DOM is a lightweight JavaScript description of the UI. It lets you write declarative code (describe the result, not the steps) while React works out the minimal real DOM changes and batches them. The win is predictable, batched updates without hand-written DOM code, not raw speed. Frameworks like Svelte and Solid skip the virtual DOM entirely.`,hook:`📝 The virtual DOM is a draft you can scribble on freely; React only copies the differences onto the real page.`,tags:[`virtual-dom`,`rendering`]},{id:`ri-jsx`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What is JSX, and what does it compile to?`,text:`JSX is syntax that looks like HTML inside JavaScript. It compiles to function calls that create element objects (jsx(type, props), formerly React.createElement). It exists so you don't write those nested calls by hand.`,hook:`🍬 JSX is sugar: sweet to write, but underneath it's just function calls returning plain objects.`,tags:[`jsx`]},{id:`ri-rerender-triggers`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What triggers a re-render?`,text:`Three things: a state update in that component (useState/useReducer), its parent re-rendering, or a context it consumes changing. "Props changed" isn't a separate trigger, since props change because the parent re-rendered. By default a parent's re-render renders all its children unless they're memoised.`,hook:`🔔 Three bells ring a re-render: my own state, my parent, my context.`,tags:[`rendering`,`state`]},{id:`ri-render-vs-commit`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What are the render phase and the commit phase?`,text:`Render: React calls your components and diffs the result against the previous tree. It must be pure, and it can be paused, restarted, thrown away or run more than once. Commit: React applies the DOM changes in one go, attaches refs, runs useLayoutEffect synchronously, lets the browser paint, then runs useEffect. That's why side effects never go in the render body.`,hook:`✍️ Render is writing the plan in pencil (erasable); commit is pouring the concrete (once, all at once).`,tags:[`rendering`,`effects`]},{id:`ri-reconciliation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`How does reconciliation (the diffing algorithm) work?`,text:`A general tree diff is O(n³), so React uses two rules to make it O(n). Different element type at the same position (div → span, A → B): tear down the whole subtree and build a new one, losing state. Same type: keep the DOM node or component instance and update only the changed props. For lists of children, keys match items between renders.`,hook:`🔁 Same type, same spot = renovate. Different type = demolish and rebuild.`,tags:[`reconciliation`,`rendering`]},{id:`ri-keys`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`How do keys work, and why not use the array index?`,text:`Without keys React matches children by position, so inserting at the top makes every item look changed. With keys it matches by identity: it moves existing nodes, creates new ones and removes missing ones. Keys must be stable and unique among siblings. Index keys break on reorder, insert or delete, because state and DOM get attached to the wrong item.`,hook:`🏷️ A key is a name tag; an index is a seat number, and seats change when someone new sits down.`,tags:[`reconciliation`,`lists`]},{id:`ri-state-preservation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`When does a component keep its state, and when does it lose it?`,text:`State lives at a position in the tree. The same type at the same position keeps it, even across a ternary ({a ? <Counter/ : <Counter/} shares one state). A different type, a different key, or the component moving position resets it. Classic bug: defining a component inside another component creates a new type every render, so it remounts and loses state every time.`,hook:`📍 State is tied to the seat, not the person: same seat, same state; new seat or new type, fresh start.`,tags:[`state`,`reconciliation`]},{id:`ri-fiber`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What is Fiber?`,text:`React's reconciliation engine since React 16. A fiber is a unit of work, one per component or element, linked by child, sibling and parent pointers, so React walks the tree in a loop instead of recursion. That lets it pause, resume, prioritise or abandon rendering. React keeps two trees, current (on screen) and work-in-progress, and swaps them on commit. Before Fiber, reconciliation was synchronous and recursive, so a big update blocked the main thread and dropped frames. Fiber is the foundation that Suspense and transitions are built on.`,hook:`🧶 Old React knitted the whole scarf in one go; Fiber knits a row, checks if you need it to stop, then carries on.`,tags:[`fiber`,`concurrency`]},{id:`ri-concurrent-rendering`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What does concurrent rendering actually do?`,text:`Rendering becomes interruptible. React works in small slices (about 5ms), yields to the browser so input and paint stay smooth, and can drop a stale render if newer state arrives. Urgent updates (typing) take priority over transitions (filtering a big list). The commit phase is still synchronous, so you never see a half-finished UI.`,hook:`🚑 Concurrent React lets the ambulance (your keystroke) overtake the delivery truck (the big re-render).`,tags:[`concurrency`,`performance`]},{id:`ri-tearing`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What is "tearing", and how does React prevent it?`,text:`During an interruptible render, an external store can change midway, so different components show different versions of the same data. useSyncExternalStore prevents it by forcing a consistent snapshot of the store for the whole render.`,hook:`📸 Tearing is half the class photo taken before the teacher moved and half after.`,tags:[`concurrency`,`external-store`]},{id:`ri-setstate-batching`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:"Why does `setState` seem asynchronous?",text:`Updates are queued and batched, and the state you read during a render is a snapshot for that render. So calling setCount(count + 1) twice adds 1, not 2. Use the updater form setCount(c = c + 1) when the next state depends on the previous one. flushSync forces an immediate update but is rarely needed.`,hook:`📮 setState drops a letter in the post box; you won't see the reply until the next render opens the mail.`,tags:[`state`,`batching`]},{id:`ri-effect-order`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What's the order of effects?`,text:`In the commit phase: DOM mutations, then refs attached, then layout effects (children before parents), then the browser paints, then passive effects (useEffect, children before parents). On re-run or unmount, the previous cleanup runs before the new effect.`,hook:`👶 Kids before parents, layout before paint, paint before passive effects, and always clean up before you set up again.`,tags:[`effects`,`lifecycle`]},{id:`ri-synthetic-events`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`How does React handle events?`,text:`React uses event delegation: one listener per event type on the root container (since React 17; on document before that), which dispatches synthetic events, a cross-browser wrapper over native events. Updates triggered inside one event handler are batched into one render.`,hook:`☎️ One receptionist at the front desk (the root) takes every call and forwards it to the right component.`,tags:[`events`]},{id:`ri-hydration`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`Explain hydration in terms of the DOM.`,text:`Server rendering sends real HTML, so users see content before any JavaScript runs. Hydration then runs the components on the client and attaches to the existing DOM instead of recreating it, wiring up events and state. If the client's first render differs from the server HTML, you get a hydration mismatch. React 18's selective hydration lets Suspense boundaries hydrate independently, prioritising whichever one the user interacts with first.`,hook:`💧 SSR delivers the dry sponge (HTML); hydration adds water (events and state) without reshaping it.`,tags:[`ssr`,`hydration`]},{id:`ri-rsc-payload`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`What's the difference between the RSC payload and SSR HTML?`,text:`SSR HTML is for the first paint. The RSC payload is a serialised description of the Server Component tree (with placeholders for Client Components) that React uses to build and update the tree on the client. On client-side navigation, Next.js fetches only the RSC payload, not HTML.`,hook:`🧾 HTML is the printed page; the RSC payload is the editable blueprint React keeps updating from.`,tags:[`server-components`,`ssr`]},{id:`ri-avoid-rerenders`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-internals`,title:`How do you stop unnecessary re-renders, in order of preference?`,text:`First, move state down, closer to where it's used. Second, lift content up and pass it as children, because children created by the parent don't re-render when the wrapper's state changes. Third, split contexts. Last, memo with stable props (useMemo/useCallback, or the React Compiler). Measure with the Profiler before and after.`,hook:`⬇️ Move state down, lift content up, split context, memo last.`,tags:[`performance`,`memoisation`]},{id:`next-file-routing`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`How does file-based routing work?`,text:`A file's path under app/ becomes its URL, so app/about/page.tsx is /about, with no router config to maintain. A folder in square brackets like app/products/[id]/page.tsx creates a dynamic route where id comes from the URL.`,hook:`🗂️ The folder tree is the sitemap.`,tags:[`routing`,`app-router`]},{id:`next-app-vs-pages`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`App Router vs Pages Router?`,text:`The App Router defaults to Server Components, has nested layouts that persist across navigation, loading/error files, streaming, and data fetching directly inside components with async/await. The Pages Router renders Client Components and fetches through special functions like getServerSideProps/getStaticProps.`,hook:`🏢 Pages Router: every page is its own house. App Router: apartments sharing a lobby (layouts) that never gets rebuilt.`,tags:[`app-router`,`routing`]},{id:`next-server-vs-client`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`Server vs Client Components: how do you decide?`,text:`Default to Server Components: they ship no JavaScript and can read data directly. Add "use client" only where you need state, effects, event handlers, browser APIs or a browser-only library, and keep that boundary as low in the tree as possible.`,hook:`🏝️ The page is a server-rendered ocean with small interactive islands.`,tags:[`server-components`]},{id:`next-rendering-strategies`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`SSR vs SSG vs ISR vs CSR: how do you pick?`,text:`SSG builds once, for content that rarely changes (marketing, docs). SSR renders per request, for fresh per-user data (dashboards). ISR is static but revalidates on a timer or on demand (blogs, product listings). CSR renders in the browser, for highly interactive screens behind a login.`,hook:`🍞 SSG = bake once, SSR = bake to order, ISR = bake and refresh the shelf every so often, CSR = DIY kit.`,tags:[`rendering`,`ssr`]},{id:`next-protect-routes`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`How do you protect a route?`,text:`Middleware redirects unauthenticated requests early. Also check on the server (in the layout, page or Server Action), because middleware alone isn't a security boundary, and remember the real check is the API's: anything the client can skip, an attacker can skip too.`,hook:`🚪 Middleware is the doorman; the server check is the lock on the safe.`,tags:[`auth`,`middleware`]},{id:`next-layout-page-template`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`Layout vs page vs template?`,text:`Layouts persist across navigation and keep their state. Pages remount when you navigate to them. Templates remount on every navigation even when shared, which is useful for per-page enter animations or resetting state. Long-lived things like a WebSocket connection belong in a layout, so navigating between child pages doesn't reconnect.`,hook:`🖼️ The layout is the frame that stays on the wall; pages are the pictures you swap in.`,tags:[`app-router`,`routing`]},{id:`next-caching`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`Walk through Next.js's caching layers.`,text:`Request memoisation dedupes identical fetch calls within one render. The Data Cache keeps fetch results across requests and deploys until revalidated. The Full Route Cache stores rendered HTML/RSC for static routes. The client Router Cache keeps visited segments for instant back/forward. "I updated the DB but the page is stale" is almost always the Data Cache; fix it with revalidatePath, revalidateTag or cache: 'no-store'.`,hook:`🧅 Four layers of onion: this render, the server's data, the built page, the browser's memory. Peel the right one.`,tags:[`caching`,`performance`]},{id:`next-revalidate`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:"`revalidatePath` vs `revalidateTag`?",text:`revalidatePath invalidates everything cached for one route: simple but coarse. revalidateTag invalidates every fetch tagged with a name, across all routes that use it, e.g. a product shown on both a listing and a detail page.`,hook:`🏷️ Path = clear one room; tag = clear that item from every room it appears in.`,tags:[`caching`]},{id:`next-hydration-errors`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`What causes hydration errors?`,text:`The server HTML differs from the client's first render. Classic causes: Date.now() or Math.random() during render, reading window/localStorage during render, locale-dependent formatting, and invalid HTML nesting (a <div inside a <p). Fix it by moving client-only values into an effect, or rendering them only after mount.`,hook:`🧬 Server and client must draw the same first picture; any random, time or browser-only value makes them disagree.`,tags:[`hydration`,`ssr`]},{id:`next-actions-vs-handlers`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`Server Actions vs Route Handlers?`,text:`A Server Action ("use server") is a function a component calls directly like an RPC, which is good for form submissions and mutations tied to the UI. A Route Handler is a real HTTP endpoint, needed for webhooks, third-party integrations or APIs other clients will call.`,hook:`📞 Actions are internal phone extensions; Route Handlers are the public phone number.`,tags:[`server-actions`,`api`]},{id:`next-env-vars`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`How do environment variables work in Next.js?`,text:`Only variables prefixed NEXTPUBLIC are inlined into the browser bundle; everything else stays on the server. Never put secrets in NEXTPUBLIC, because anyone can read them in the JS.`,hook:`📣 NEXTPUBLIC means "shout it to the whole internet".`,tags:[`config`,`security`]},{id:`next-builtins`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:"What do `next/image`, `next/link` and `next/font` give you?",text:`next/image: responsive sizes, lazy loading, modern formats (WebP/AVIF) and no layout shift when width and height are set. next/link: client-side navigation with background prefetching, with no full reload, unlike a plain <a. next/font: self-hosted fonts with matched fallback metrics, so no layout shift when they swap in.`,hook:`🧰 Image fixes weight, Link fixes waiting, Font fixes jumping.`,tags:[`performance`,`images`,`fonts`]},{id:`next-dynamic-routes`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`Dynamic routes and static params?`,text:`app/posts/[id]/page.tsx handles any id. generateStaticParams prebuilds known ids at build time, and generateMetadata produces per-page titles and descriptions for SEO.`,hook:`🧱 One mould ([id]), many bricks: generateStaticParams decides which bricks are fired in advance.`,tags:[`routing`,`ssg`,`seo`]},{id:`next-middleware`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`Middleware: what is it for, and what are its limits?`,text:`Middleware runs before routing, on the Edge runtime: auth redirects, i18n, A/B bucketing. Limits: no full Node APIs, many npm packages won't work, and it adds latency to every matched request, so keep the matcher narrow.`,hook:`🛂 Middleware is passport control: quick checks only, and only for the flights that need them.`,tags:[`middleware`,`edge`]},{id:`next-streaming`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`How does streaming with Suspense work?`,text:`Wrapping a slow, data-dependent section in <Suspense (or adding loading.tsx) lets Next.js send the fast parts immediately and stream the slow part in when it's ready, instead of holding the whole response until the slowest query finishes.`,hook:`🍽️ Serve the starter while the main course is still cooking.`,tags:[`streaming`,`suspense`,`performance`]},{id:`next-seo`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`nextjs`,title:`How does Next.js help SEO, and what's still on you?`,text:`Server-rendered HTML means crawlers see real content without running JavaScript, and the Metadata API handles title, description and Open Graph tags per route. You still own structured data (JSON-LD), canonical URLs, sitemaps, and making sure dynamic routes get real metadata, not a generic fallback.`,hook:`🔎 Next.js opens the door for crawlers; you still have to label the rooms.`,tags:[`seo`,`metadata`]},{id:`ts-type-vs-interface`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`typescript`,title:"`type` vs `interface`?",text:`Both describe object shapes. Interfaces can be extended and support declaration merging (reopening to add fields), which suits public API shapes. type is more flexible: unions, intersections, mapped and conditional types. Pick one convention for props and stay consistent.`,hook:`📐 interface is a blueprint you can extend; type is a Swiss-army knife that can also describe "this OR that".`,tags:[`types`]},{id:`ts-generics`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`typescript`,title:`Why use generics? Show a typed API client.`,text:`Generics make reusable code that keeps type information instead of collapsing to any. A typed fetch lets each call site say what shape it expects, so if the API type changes, every caller that uses a missing field breaks at compile time instead of in production. The as cast still trusts the server. Pair it with a runtime schema (Zod) at the boundary so the type is actually true.`,hook:`📦 A generic is a labelled box: whatever type goes in, the same type comes out.`,tags:[`generics`]},{id:`ts-utility-types`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`typescript`,title:`Name some utility types.`,text:`Partial, Required, Readonly, Pick, Omit, Record, ReturnType, Parameters, Awaited, NonNullable. They transform existing types so you don't duplicate them.`,hook:`🛠️ Utility types are type-level array methods: pick, omit, map, unwrap.`,tags:[`utility-types`]},{id:`ts-any-unknown-never`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`typescript`,title:"`any` vs `unknown` vs `never`?",text:`any switches type-checking off. unknown is the safe "could be anything": you must narrow it before use. never means "can't happen", and it's used for exhaustive checks so adding a new union member becomes a compile error.`,hook:`🚫 any = no rules, unknown = rules apply until you check, never = this door is bricked up.`,tags:[`types`,`narrowing`]},{id:`ts-discriminated-unions`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`typescript`,title:`How do you make invalid states unrepresentable?`,text:`Use a discriminated union with a shared literal field instead of separate booleans. Three booleans (isLoading, isError, data) allow impossible combos like "loading and has data". A union on status makes TypeScript narrow automatically, so data only exists in the success branch. Most mid-level answers reach for the boolean soup; naming this pattern is a clear senior signal.`,hook:`🚦 One traffic light with three colours beats three separate bulbs that can all be on at once.`,tags:[`unions`,`narrowing`,`state`]},{id:`ts-keyof-typeof-satisfies`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`typescript`,title:"`keyof`, `typeof`, `as const`, `satisfies`?",text:`keyof T is the union of T's keys. typeof value lifts a value's type into the type world. as const makes literals narrow and readonly. satisfies checks that a value matches a type without widening it, so you keep the precise inferred type.`,hook:`🔑 keyof lists the keys, typeof borrows a shape, as const freezes it, satisfies checks it without squashing it.`,tags:[`types`,`inference`]},{id:`ts-enums-vs-unions`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`typescript`,title:`Enums vs a union of string literals?`,text:`Prefer string literal unions ('a' | 'b'): no runtime code, and they work directly with plain strings from APIs. Enums generate JavaScript, have numeric-enum quirks, and don't fit "types are erased" tooling like Node's type stripping.`,hook:`🧾 A union is just a list on paper; an enum builds a whole object you have to ship.`,tags:[`types`]},{id:`ts-runtime-validation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`typescript`,title:`Types are erased at runtime. How do you validate data?`,text:`TypeScript can't check API responses, form input or localStorage at runtime. Use a schema library like Zod at the boundary and infer the type from the schema, so the runtime check and the static type can never drift apart.`,hook:`🛃 TypeScript checks your luggage at home; Zod checks it again at the border.`,tags:[`validation`,`zod`]},{id:`ts-this-callbacks`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`typescript`,title:"`this` in regular vs arrow functions in TypeScript callbacks?",text:`A regular function's this depends on the call site, so passing a method as a callback can lose it. An arrow function captures this lexically from where it's defined. TypeScript can flag the problem when noImplicitThis is on and you annotate this parameters.`,hook:`👉 Arrow functions remember where they were born; regular ones only know who's holding them right now.`,tags:[`functions`,`this`]},{id:`css-semantic-html`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:`Why does semantic HTML matter?`,text:`Screen readers use semantic elements to navigate (headings, landmarks, lists), search engines use them to understand the page, and you get built-in behaviour for free: a <button is focusable and responds to Enter and Space; a <div onClick does neither.`,hook:`🏷️ Semantic tags are labels on the boxes: people with screen readers, and Google, can't see inside otherwise.`,tags:[`html`,`accessibility`,`seo`]},{id:`css-box-model`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:"Explain the box model and `box-sizing`.",text:`Every element is content, then padding, then border, then margin. By default width measures only the content, so padding and border make the box bigger. box-sizing: border-box makes width include padding and border, which is why resets set it globally.`,hook:`🖼️ Picture (content), mount (padding), frame (border), gap on the wall (margin). border-box sizes the whole framed picture.`,tags:[`css`,`layout`]},{id:`css-specificity-layers`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:"CSS specificity, and what does `@layer` solve?",text:`Specificity ranks inline styles, then ids, then classes/attributes/pseudo-classes, then elements. On a tie, the later rule wins; !important overrides everything and should be a last resort. @layer orders whole groups of rules (reset, base, components, utilities) so a later layer beats an earlier one regardless of selector specificity, which ends specificity wars with libraries.`,hook:`🏆 Specificity is a points table; @layer is a league system where the higher league always wins, whatever the score.`,tags:[`css`,`cascade`]},{id:`css-flex-vs-grid`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:`Flexbox vs Grid?`,text:`Flexbox is one-dimensional (a row or a column) and content-driven. Grid is two-dimensional (rows and columns) and layout-driven. A common pattern is Grid for the page and Flexbox inside components.`,hook:`📏 Flex is a ruler (one line), Grid is graph paper (rows and columns).`,tags:[`css`,`layout`]},{id:`css-flex-axes`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:"What do `justify-content` and `align-items` control?",text:`justify-content positions items along the main axis (horizontal in a row) and align-items along the cross axis (vertical in a row). Switch to flex-direction: column and the axes swap.`,hook:`↔️ Justify follows the direction of travel; align goes across it.`,tags:[`css`,`flexbox`]},{id:`css-centering`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:`How do you centre a div?`,text:`display: grid; place-items: center; on the parent, or flex with justify-content: center; align-items: center;. For an absolutely positioned element with a known size: inset: 0; margin: auto;.`,hook:`🎯 Grid + place-items: center: two lines, done.`,tags:[`css`,`layout`]},{id:`css-position`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:"The five `position` values?",text:`static is normal flow. relative offsets the element but keeps its original space. absolute leaves the flow and positions against the nearest positioned ancestor. fixed positions against the viewport and stays put on scroll. sticky behaves like relative until a scroll threshold, then sticks like fixed.`,hook:`📌 Static sits, relative nudges, absolute floats to its parent, fixed glues to the screen, sticky sticks when you scroll past.`,tags:[`css`,`layout`]},{id:`css-z-index`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:"Why doesn't my `z-index` work?",text:`z-index only competes within the same stacking context. transform, opacity < 1, filter, isolation: isolate, or a positioned element with a z-index each create a new context, and children can't escape their parent's layer no matter how big their z-index is.`,hook:`🏢 z-index is a floor number inside one building; you can't climb above a taller building next door.`,tags:[`css`,`stacking`]},{id:`css-units`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:"`em`, `rem`, `%`, `vh`/`dvh`?",text:`rem is relative to the root font size: predictable. em is relative to the parent's font size and compounds when nested. % is relative to the parent's size. On mobile use dvh instead of vh, because 100vh includes the area behind the browser toolbar and overflows.`,hook:`🌳 rem measures from the root; em measures from your parent, and parents of parents multiply.`,tags:[`css`,`responsive`]},{id:`css-hiding`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:"`display: none` vs `visibility: hidden` vs `opacity: 0`?",text:`display: none removes the element from layout and the accessibility tree. visibility: hidden keeps its space but hides it from sight and screen readers. opacity: 0 keeps it in place, still clickable and still announced.`,hook:`👻 None = gone, hidden = invisible but taking a seat, opacity 0 = a ghost you can still bump into.`,tags:[`css`,`accessibility`]},{id:`css-responsive`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:`What's your approach to responsive design?`,text:`Mobile-first: base styles for the smallest screen, then min-width media queries as space grows. Use fluid units (rem, %, fr), clamp() for type, and container queries for components that live in slots of different widths.`,hook:`📱 Start small and add; it's easier to add rooms to a cottage than knock walls out of a mansion.`,tags:[`css`,`responsive`]},{id:`css-container-queries`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:`Container queries vs media queries?`,text:`Media queries respond to the viewport; container queries respond to the size of the component's container. Use container queries when the same card must lay out differently in a wide main column and a narrow sidebar on the same screen.`,hook:`🪟 Media queries look out the window; container queries look at the box they're sitting in.`,tags:[`css`,`responsive`]},{id:`css-animations`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:`Transitions vs animations, and how do you keep them smooth?`,text:`Transitions animate between two states (e.g. on hover); keyframe animations can loop and have multiple steps. Animate transform and opacity, which are composited, rather than width/top, which trigger layout. Respect prefers-reduced-motion.`,hook:`🎞️ Transition = A to B; animation = a whole storyboard. Either way, move with transform, not with width.`,tags:[`css`,`animation`,`performance`]},{id:`css-pseudo`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:`Pseudo-classes vs pseudo-elements?`,text:`Pseudo-classes (:hover, :focus-visible, :has()) select an element in a state. Pseudo-elements (::before, ::after, ::placeholder) style a part of an element, or insert generated content.`,hook:`🎭 One colon = a mood the element is in; two colons = a piece of the element.`,tags:[`css`,`selectors`]},{id:`css-tailwind`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:`Tailwind vs plain CSS: what's the trade-off?`,text:`Tailwind gives co-located styles, a constrained design system that prevents drift across a team, and no dead CSS. The cost is long class strings and a learning curve. Plain CSS/Sass keeps separation of concerns and can be clearer for complex selectors or animations. On a team shipping fast, Tailwind's consistency usually wins.`,hook:`🧱 Tailwind is LEGO with a fixed set of bricks: less freedom, no mismatched pieces.`,tags:[`css`,`tailwind`,`tooling`]},{id:`css-cross-browser`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`html-css`,title:`Tell me about a cross-browser bug you've hit.`,text:`Have a real one ready. A common one is mobile Safari's 100vh including the area behind the address bar, causing overflow; the fix is 100dvh. Name the browser, the symptom, how you isolated it (DevTools device mode, BrowserStack, a real device), and the fix.`,hook:`🐞 Browser, symptom, isolate, fix: tell it like a bug report.`,tags:[`css`,`debugging`,`browsers`]},{id:`web-storage`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`web-fundamentals`,title:"`localStorage` vs `sessionStorage` vs cookies?",text:`localStorage persists per origin (about 5 MB) until cleared. sessionStorage lasts for one tab. Cookies are small (about 4 KB), sent with every matching request, and can be HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite. Only cookies reach the server automatically.`,hook:`🧳 localStorage is a locker, sessionStorage is a coat check for one visit, cookies are a badge you flash at every door.`,tags:[`storage`,`cookies`]},{id:`web-cors`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`web-fundamentals`,title:`What is CORS?`,text:`A browser rule: a page can only read a cross-origin response if the server allows it via Access-Control-Allow-Origin. Non-simple requests (custom headers, JSON bodies, PUT/DELETE) send a preflight OPTIONS first. It's enforced by the browser and fixed on the server, never in client code.`,hook:`🛂 CORS is the destination country's visa policy: the traveller (browser) checks it, but only the country (server) can change it.`,tags:[`cors`,`security`,`http`]},{id:`web-http-caching`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`web-fundamentals`,title:`How does HTTP caching work?`,text:`Cache-Control sets the rules: max-age (fresh for N seconds), no-cache (revalidate before using), no-store (never store), immutable. ETag/Last-Modified let the browser revalidate and get a cheap 304 Not Modified. Put content hashes in static asset file names so they can be cached forever.`,hook:`🥛 max-age is the best-before date; an ETag is asking the shop "is this still the latest batch?"`,tags:[`caching`,`http`,`performance`]},{id:`web-workers`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`web-fundamentals`,title:`Web Workers vs Service Workers?`,text:`A Web Worker runs heavy computation off the main thread so the UI stays responsive. A Service Worker sits between the page and the network as a programmable proxy, enabling offline support, caching strategies and push notifications. It's the core of a PWA.`,hook:`👷 A Web Worker is an extra pair of hands in the kitchen; a Service Worker is the doorman who can serve from the pantry when the shop is closed.`,tags:[`workers`,`pwa`,`performance`]},{id:`web-realtime`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`web-fundamentals`,title:`Polling vs SSE vs WebSockets: how do you choose?`,text:`Polling is simplest and fine when a few seconds' lag is OK. Server-Sent Events stream one-way, server to client, over plain HTTP, with built-in reconnection (notifications, tickers). WebSockets are two-way and stateful (chat, multiplayer), but you manage reconnection and missed messages yourself. With WebSockets, plan for reconnects: resubscribe, fetch what you missed since the last message id, and dedupe.`,hook:`📬 Polling = checking the mailbox, SSE = a radio station, WebSockets = a phone call.`,tags:[`realtime`,`websockets`,`sse`]},{id:`web-https`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`web-fundamentals`,title:`Why does HTTPS matter, beyond "it's more secure"?`,text:`Without it, everything between browser and server, including passwords, tokens and cookies, travels as plain text that anyone on the network path can read or alter. HTTPS encrypts the traffic and verifies the server's identity with a certificate, which stops both eavesdropping and impersonation. Many browser APIs (service workers, geolocation, clipboard) also require it.`,hook:`✉️ HTTP is a postcard anyone can read; HTTPS is a sealed, signed envelope.`,tags:[`security`,`tls`]},{id:`state-why`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:`What problem does state management solve?`,text:`Once an app grows, passing everything through props means data travels through every intermediate component to reach the ones that need it. State management lets components read and update shared data directly, through Context, a store, or a query library for server data.`,hook:`📡 Props are passing notes down a row of desks; state management is a noticeboard everyone can read.`,tags:[`state`,`architecture`]},{id:`state-kinds`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:`Local vs global vs server state?`,text:`Local state belongs to one component (useState). Global UI state is client state shared widely (theme, modals) via Context or a store. Server state is a cache of backend data: it goes stale, needs refetching and deduping, so use a query library. Most "global state" problems are really server state.`,hook:`🏠 Local is your room, global is the shared living room, server state is groceries that go off.`,tags:[`state`,`server-state`]},{id:`state-usestate-vs-usereducer`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:"`useState` vs `useReducer`: when do you switch?",text:`useState is fine for independent, simple values. useReducer earns its keep when several values change together in response to one action, or when the next-state logic is complex: centralising it in a pure reducer makes transitions explicit and easy to unit-test.`,hook:`🎛️ Many independent knobs: useState. One control panel where a button moves several knobs: useReducer.`,tags:[`hooks`,`reducers`]},{id:`state-lifting`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:`What does "lifting state up" mean?`,text:`When two siblings need the same state, move it to their nearest common parent and pass it down as props. Try this before reaching for Context or a global store.`,hook:`🧗 If two siblings need the same toy, give it to the parent and let them share.`,tags:[`state`,`props`]},{id:`state-libraries`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:`When do you reach for Redux, Zustand or TanStack Query?`,text:`Redux Toolkit for large apps with complex shared client state and devtools/time-travel needs. Zustand for the same with far less boilerplate. TanStack Query for server data: caching, background refetch, dedupe, retries. Context stops being enough when updates are frequent, because every consumer re-renders. Be honest about which you've used in production. Saying "I know the model but haven't shipped Zustand" beats guessing at APIs live.`,hook:`🧰 Redux = the full workshop, Zustand = a good toolbox, TanStack Query = a specialist for data that lives on the server.`,tags:[`redux`,`zustand`,`tanstack-query`]},{id:`state-redux-flow`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:`Explain the Redux data flow.`,text:`A component dispatches an action (a plain object describing what happened). A pure reducer takes the current state and the action and returns new state, never mutating the old one. The store notifies subscribers, and useSelector re-renders only the components whose selected slice changed.`,hook:`🔄 Dispatch → reduce → notify → select: tell it what happened, it decides what changes, the right people hear about it.`,tags:[`redux`]},{id:`state-normalisation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:`Why normalise state?`,text:`Nested, duplicated data (each post embedding its full author) means updating one author requires finding every copy, and missing one leaves inconsistent UI. Normalising stores each entity once by id in flat lookup tables, with references by id: one update, one place.`,hook:`📇 Keep one master contact card per person, and have everything else point to it.`,tags:[`redux`,`data-modelling`]},{id:`state-tanstack-query`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:`What does TanStack Query solve that Redux doesn't?`,text:`It's built for server state: caching, background refetching, stale-while-revalidate, request deduplication, retries and invalidation after mutations. Teams often rebuild a worse version of this inside Redux (loading flags, manual cache busting) before adopting it.`,hook:`🥫 Redux stores what you own; TanStack Query manages what you've borrowed from the server and must keep fresh.`,tags:[`tanstack-query`,`server-state`]},{id:`state-staletime-gctime`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:"`staleTime` vs `gcTime`?",text:`staleTime is how long data counts as fresh, so refocusing the window won't refetch it. gcTime (formerly cacheTime) is how long unused data stays in memory after nothing subscribes to it. After a mutation, call invalidateQueries on the affected keys.`,hook:`🍞 staleTime = how long the bread is fresh; gcTime = how long you keep it before throwing it out.`,tags:[`tanstack-query`,`caching`]},{id:`state-optimistic-updates`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`state-management`,title:`How do you implement an optimistic update?`,text:`Snapshot the current state, apply the change immediately so the UI feels instant, send the request, then reconcile with the server's response. On failure, roll back to the snapshot (or mark the item failed with a retry) and tell the user. The failure path is the part most answers skip, and the part that matters.`,hook:`🤞 Act as if it worked, keep a receipt, and refund cleanly if it didn't.`,tags:[`ux`,`mutations`]},{id:`perf-slow-react`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`performance`,title:`How do you find and fix a slow React app?`,text:`Measure first: React DevTools Profiler, the Chrome Performance panel, Lighthouse. Then fix what you find: cut unnecessary re-renders (state placement, memo, split contexts), virtualise long lists, code-split heavy routes, debounce expensive handlers, and move heavy work into useTransition or a Web Worker.`,hook:`🩺 Diagnose before you prescribe: profile first, memo later.`,tags:[`react`,`profiling`]},{id:`perf-core-web-vitals`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`performance`,title:`Core Web Vitals: what causes each, and how do you fix it?`,text:`LCP (largest contentful paint, ≤ 2.5s): usually a slow hero image or render-blocking resources; preload it and cut blocking CSS/JS. INP (interaction to next paint, ≤ 200ms): long tasks and heavy handlers; break up work or defer it. CLS (layout shift, ≤ 0.1): images without dimensions, late banners, font swaps; reserve space up front.`,hook:`⏱️ LCP = how fast it shows up, INP = how fast it answers, CLS = whether it sits still.`,tags:[`web-vitals`,`lcp`,`inp`,`cls`]},{id:`perf-bundle-size`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`performance`,title:`How do you reduce bundle size, and what breaks tree-shaking?`,text:`Analyse the bundle, then code-split, lazy-load, replace heavy libraries and import only what you use. Tree-shaking needs static ES modules; it silently breaks with CommonJS packages, barrel files that re-export everything, and a missing or wrong sideEffects field in package.json.`,hook:`🌳 Tree-shaking only drops leaves the bundler can prove are dead; CommonJS and barrels hide the proof.`,tags:[`bundling`,`tree-shaking`]},{id:`perf-long-lists`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`performance`,title:`How do you render a list of 10,000 items?`,text:`Virtualisation (react-window, TanStack Virtual) renders only the rows in view plus a small buffer. Combine it with pagination or infinite scroll and stable keys. Note that virtualisation complicates scroll restoration, find-in-page and accessibility, so don't use it for lists that are short anyway.`,hook:`🪟 Only furnish the rooms people can see through the window.`,tags:[`virtualisation`,`lists`]},{id:`perf-images`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`performance`,title:`How do you optimise images?`,text:`Serve the right dimensions (not a 4000px original scaled down by CSS), modern formats (WebP/AVIF), srcset/sizes for responsive variants, lazy-load anything below the fold, give the LCP image priority, and always set width and height to prevent layout shift. Images are often the biggest, cheapest win on a page.`,hook:`🖼️ Right size, right format, right time, and always reserve the frame.`,tags:[`images`,`lcp`]},{id:`perf-lazy-loading`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`performance`,title:`What is lazy loading?`,text:`Deferring the loading of something (a component, an image, a route's code) until it's actually needed. A dynamic import() for a modal's contents, or loading="lazy" on below-the-fold images, means you don't pay download and parse costs for things the user may never see.`,hook:`🛋️ Don't carry the sofa upstairs until someone wants to sit on it.`,tags:[`lazy-loading`]},{id:`a11y-basics`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`accessibility`,title:`What makes a site accessible?`,text:`Semantic HTML, full keyboard operability with a visible focus state, text contrast of at least 4.5:1, labels on every input, meaningful alt text, a sensible heading order, ARIA only where HTML can't express something, and respect for prefers-reduced-motion.`,hook:`⌨️ If you can't use it with only a keyboard, and a screen reader can't describe it, it isn't done.`,tags:[`wcag`,`semantics`]},{id:`a11y-aria`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`accessibility`,title:`When should you use ARIA?`,text:`As a last resort. The first rule of ARIA is to use a native element if one exists. Legitimate uses include aria-label on icon-only buttons, aria-live for status updates, aria-expanded on toggles, and role="dialog" with aria-modal. Wrong ARIA is worse than none.`,hook:`🩹 ARIA is a plaster for what HTML can't do, not a replacement for the right element.`,tags:[`aria`]},{id:`a11y-modal`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`accessibility`,title:`How do you build an accessible modal?`,text:`Move focus into the modal on open and trap Tab inside it. Close on Escape. Return focus to the element that opened it. Use role="dialog" with aria-modal="true" and a label, and make the background inert so screen readers can't wander behind it. The native <dialog element with showModal() handles much of this. Most "accessible modal" bugs in practice are a missing focus trap or focus not returning to the trigger.`,hook:`🔐 Focus in, focus trapped, Escape out, focus back home.`,tags:[`focus`,`dialogs`]},{id:`a11y-alt-text`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`accessibility`,title:`Why does alt text matter, and when is it empty?`,text:`Screen readers announce alt text in place of the image, so it should convey what the image means in context. Search engines use it too. Purely decorative images get alt="" (empty, not missing) so screen readers skip them instead of reading a file name.`,hook:`🗣️ Describe what the image says, not what it is; if it says nothing, say nothing (alt="").`,tags:[`images`,`seo`]},{id:`a11y-testing`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`accessibility`,title:`How do you test accessibility?`,text:`Automated checks (axe, Lighthouse) catch roughly a third of issues, so add a keyboard-only pass (Tab through everything, check focus order and visibility) and a screen-reader pass (NVDA on Windows, VoiceOver on Mac/iOS). Wire axe into end-to-end tests so regressions fail CI.`,hook:`🤖👤 Robots find the obvious third; your keyboard and a screen reader find the rest.`,tags:[`testing`,`tooling`]},{id:`test-pyramid`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`testing`,title:`Unit vs integration vs end-to-end tests?`,text:`Unit tests check a pure function or reducer in isolation, and they're fast. Integration tests check components working together with the network mocked (React Testing Library). End-to-end tests drive a real browser through user flows (Playwright). Weight them by risk: lots of fast unit tests, fewer slow E2E tests on critical paths.`,hook:`🔺 Many cheap bricks at the base, a few expensive windows at the top.`,tags:[`strategy`]},{id:`test-rtl`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`testing`,title:`What's React Testing Library's philosophy?`,text:`Test behaviour the way a user experiences it: query by role, label or text, interact, and assert on what's visible. Don't test implementation details like internal state or class names, so refactors don't break tests that should still pass.`,hook:`👀 Test what the user sees and does, not how the component is wired inside.`,tags:[`react`,`rtl`]},{id:`test-mocking`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`testing`,title:`How do you mock API calls?`,text:`Mock Service Worker (MSW) intercepts fetch at the network level, so components run unchanged against realistic responses, including errors and slow responses. For unit tests, use vi.fn()/jest.fn() or vi.mock for a module.`,hook:`🎭 MSW is a stunt double for your backend: the code thinks it's talking to the real thing.`,tags:[`mocking`,`msw`]},{id:`test-priorities`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`testing`,title:`What do you test first when time is limited?`,text:`Pure logic whose bugs are silent (reducers, money and date calculations, permissions), then the critical user paths (sign in, checkout, the core workflow). Skip trivial rendering tests until those are covered.`,hook:`🎯 Test where a bug would be quiet and costly before testing where it would be loud and cheap.`,tags:[`strategy`]},{id:`rn-vs-web`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How does React Native differ from React on the web?`,text:`Same React model (components, hooks, state), but you render native views instead of DOM elements: <View, <Text, <Image, <Pressable instead of div/span/button. Styling is a JavaScript subset of CSS with Flexbox by default (column direction), there's no CSS cascade, and all text must sit inside <Text.`,hook:`🔁 Same brain (React), different body (native views instead of the DOM).`,tags:[`react-native`,`mobile`]},{id:`rn-expo`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`What is Expo, and when would you not use it?`,text:`Expo is a framework and toolchain around React Native: file-based routing (Expo Router), a large set of native modules, over-the-air updates, and cloud builds (EAS). With development builds and config plugins you can add custom native code too, so the old "eject" step is rarely needed. Skip it only for unusual native requirements or when an existing native app already dictates the setup.`,hook:`🧰 Expo is React Native with batteries, a charger and a delivery service included.`,tags:[`expo`,`tooling`]},{id:`rn-architecture`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How does React Native's architecture work?`,text:`Your JavaScript runs in a JS engine (Hermes) and drives native UI on the platform's main thread. The New Architecture replaces the old asynchronous JSON "bridge" with JSI (direct JS↔native calls), TurboModules (lazily loaded native modules) and Fabric (a new renderer supporting synchronous layout and concurrent React).`,hook:`🌉 The old bridge was a ferry carrying JSON letters; JSI is a direct road between JS and native.`,tags:[`react-native`,`architecture`]},{id:`rn-navigation`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How do you handle navigation in React Native?`,text:`Use React Navigation (stack, tab and drawer navigators) or Expo Router, which builds on it with file-based routes like Next.js. Screens stay mounted in a stack, so think about focus events (useFocusEffect) for refreshing data when a user comes back to a screen.`,hook:`🗂️ A navigation stack is a pile of cards: push to go deeper, pop to come back, and the cards underneath are still there.`,tags:[`navigation`,`expo-router`]},{id:`rn-lists`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How do you make long lists fast in React Native?`,text:`Never render big arrays with map inside a ScrollView; use FlatList or Shopify's FlashList, which virtualise rows. Give items stable keyExtractor keys, memoise row components, keep row renders cheap, and set getItemLayout when rows have fixed heights.`,hook:`🪟 Only build the rows that are on screen, and recycle them as they scroll away.`,tags:[`performance`,`lists`]},{id:`rn-storage`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How do you store data and handle secrets on mobile?`,text:`AsyncStorage (or the faster MMKV) for non-sensitive key-value data, SQLite for structured offline data, and the platform keychain/keystore via expo-secure-store for tokens. Never ship real secrets in the app bundle: anything in the binary can be extracted, so call a backend that holds the secret.`,hook:`🔐 The app bundle is a glass box: anything you put inside can be read.`,tags:[`storage`,`security`]},{id:`rn-testing-release`,domain:`frontend`,topic:`react-native`,title:`How do you test and release a React Native app?`,text:`Unit and component tests with Jest and React Native Testing Library, and end-to-end tests with Detox or Maestro. Build and submit with EAS (or Fastlane), and ship JavaScript-only fixes with over-the-air updates. Store review still applies to native changes.`,hook:`🚀 Native changes go through the app store; JavaScript fixes can fly over the air.`,tags:[`testing`,`release`]},{id:`api-rest`,domain:`backend`,topic:`api-design`,title:`What makes an API RESTful?`,text:`Resources (users, orders) are addressed by URLs that are nouns, and the HTTP method says what to do: GET /orders reads, POST /orders creates, PUT/PATCH /orders/5 updates, DELETE /orders/5 removes. It's stateless: every request carries everything the server needs.`,hook:`📚 URLs are the nouns, HTTP methods are the verbs, and the server has no memory between sentences.`,tags:[`rest`,`http`]},{id:`api-get-vs-post`,domain:`backend`,topic:`api-design`,title:`GET vs POST?`,text:`GET retrieves data, has no body, puts parameters in the URL, and is safe to cache, retry and bookmark. POST sends a body to create or trigger something, isn't idempotent by default, and shouldn't be cached or blindly retried.`,hook:`👀 GET looks, POST touches.`,tags:[`http`]},{id:`api-status-codes`,domain:`backend`,topic:`api-design`,title:`HTTP status codes you should know?`,text:`2xx success: 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content. 3xx redirection: 301/302, 304 Not Modified. 4xx client error: 400 bad request, 401 not authenticated, 403 not allowed, 404 not found, 409 conflict, 422 validation failed, 429 rate-limited. 5xx server error: 500, 502 bad gateway, 503 unavailable. 401 vs 403 comes up a lot: 401 means "who are you?", 403 means "I know who you are, and no". Many APIs also return a machine-readable error code; branch on that, not on the status alone.`,hook:`🔢 2 = 👍, 3 = look elsewhere, 4 = your fault, 5 = my fault.`,tags:[`http`,`errors`]},{id:`api-json`,domain:`backend`,topic:`api-design`,title:`Why is JSON the standard payload format?`,text:`It's lightweight and human-readable compared with XML, and it maps directly onto JavaScript objects and arrays, so JSON.parse/JSON.stringify are built in. Almost every language has a fast JSON parser, which made it the default across ecosystems.`,hook:`🧾 JSON is JavaScript's own object notation, so the browser already speaks it.`,tags:[`json`]},{id:`api-rest-vs-graphql`,domain:`backend`,topic:`api-design`,title:`REST vs GraphQL?`,text:`REST exposes resources at URLs and gets HTTP caching for free, but clients can over- or under-fetch. GraphQL exposes one endpoint where the client asks for exactly the fields it needs, which suits varied clients, but caching is harder and naive resolvers risk N+1 queries.`,hook:`🍱 REST is set menus at fixed addresses; GraphQL is à la carte through one door.`,tags:[`rest`,`graphql`]},{id:`api-n-plus-one`,domain:`backend`,topic:`api-design`,title:`What is the N+1 problem in GraphQL, and how do you fix it?`,text:`A resolver fetches a list, then fetches related data for each item one at a time: 100 posts become 101 queries. Fix it with DataLoader, which batches and dedupes the per-item lookups into one query per tick.`,hook:`🚌 Don't send 100 taxis for 100 passengers; send one bus.`,tags:[`graphql`,`performance`,`databases`]},{id:`api-error-handling`,domain:`backend`,topic:`api-design`,title:`How do you handle API errors cleanly on the frontend?`,text:`Distinguish three failures: network failure (offline, timeout) → retry banner; non-2xx response → show the server's message when safe; malformed payload (unexpected shape) → generic fallback and log it. Don't wrap fetch in one try/catch that treats them all the same. Note that fetch only rejects on network errors, not on 4xx/5xx.`,hook:`🚑 Couldn't reach them, they said no, or they spoke gibberish: three different problems, three different responses.`,tags:[`errors`,`fetch`]},{id:`api-idempotency`,domain:`backend`,topic:`api-design`,title:`Why does idempotency matter, and where does it show up on the frontend?`,text:`An idempotent request has the same effect no matter how many times it's sent: GET, PUT and DELETE by convention, but POST usually isn't. If a POST times out, blindly retrying can create duplicates (double charges, double orders). The fix is an idempotency key generated on the client and sent with the request, so the server can recognise and dedupe the retry.`,hook:`🛗 Pressing the lift button five times still brings one lift; make your POSTs behave like that button.`,tags:[`http`,`reliability`,`payments`]},{id:`node-single-thread`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nodejs`,title:`How does Node.js handle many requests on one thread?`,text:`Node runs your JavaScript on one thread, but I/O (network, disk, database) is non-blocking: Node hands it to the OS or libuv's thread pool and keeps serving other requests, then runs your callback when the result is ready. It's great for I/O-heavy work and bad at long CPU work, which blocks everyone. CPU-heavy tasks (image processing, big JSON transforms, crypto) belong in workerthreads, a queue worker, or another service.`,hook:`🍽️ One waiter taking orders from every table, never standing in the kitchen waiting for food.`,tags:[`event-loop`,`concurrency`]},{id:`node-event-loop-phases`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nodejs`,title:`What are the phases of the Node.js event loop?`,text:`Each loop iteration runs: timers (setTimeout/setInterval), pending callbacks, poll (new I/O events), check (setImmediate), then close callbacks. Between every callback, Node drains process.nextTick callbacks first, then Promise microtasks.`,hook:`🎡 Timers → I/O → check → close, and nextTick cuts in front of everyone at every stop.`,tags:[`event-loop`]},{id:`node-blocking`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nodejs`,title:`What blocks the event loop, and how do you detect it?`,text:`Anything synchronous and slow: CPU-heavy loops, JSON.parse on huge payloads, synchronous fs/crypto calls (readFileSync, pbkdf2Sync), catastrophic regexes. Detect it with event-loop lag metrics (perfhooks.monitorEventLoopDelay), --cpu-prof profiles, or Clinic.js.`,hook:`🚧 One slow Sync call is a lorry parked across the only lane.`,tags:[`performance`,`event-loop`]},{id:`node-streams`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nodejs`,title:`What are streams, and when do you use them?`,text:`Streams process data in chunks instead of loading it all into memory: readable, writable, duplex and transform. Use them for large files, uploads, CSV exports and proxying. pipeline() connects them and handles errors and backpressure (pausing a fast producer when the consumer is slow).`,hook:`🚰 Drink through a straw instead of swallowing the whole jug at once.`,tags:[`streams`,`memory`]},{id:`node-scaling`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nodejs`,title:`How do you scale a Node.js service across CPU cores?`,text:`One Node process uses one core for JavaScript. Run several processes, using the cluster module, PM2, or more commonly multiple containers behind a load balancer, and keep the processes stateless (sessions in Redis, files in object storage) so any instance can serve any request.`,hook:`🐑 Don't build one giant sheep; run a flock of identical ones that don't remember who they spoke to.`,tags:[`scaling`,`cluster`]},{id:`node-error-handling`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nodejs`,title:`How should errors be handled in a Node/Express API?`,text:`Catch async errors and forward them to one central error middleware that maps known errors to status codes and hides internals for unknown ones. Log with context (request id). Treat unhandledRejection and uncaughtException as fatal: log and exit, and let the process manager restart, because the process state may be corrupt.`,hook:`🧯 One fire station for the whole building, not a bucket in every room.`,tags:[`errors`,`express`]},{id:`node-middleware`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nodejs`,title:`What is middleware in Express?`,text:`A function (req, res, next) in a chain that runs before the route handler. It can read or modify the request, end the response, or call next() to pass control on. Typical uses: auth, logging, body parsing, rate limiting, CORS.`,hook:`🏭 Middleware is an assembly line: each station does one job and passes the request along.`,tags:[`express`,`middleware`]},{id:`node-modules`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nodejs`,title:`CommonJS vs ES modules in Node?`,text:`CommonJS (require, module.exports) loads synchronously and is the legacy default. ES modules (import/export) are static, support top-level await, and are enabled by "type": "module" or .mjs. Modern Node can require() most ESM, but mixing still has sharp edges, so new projects should use ESM throughout.`,hook:`🗺️ ESM declares its routes up front; CommonJS finds them as it drives.`,tags:[`modules`]},{id:`node-config-secrets`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nodejs`,title:`How do you keep secrets and config in a Node app?`,text:`Read config from environment variables (12-factor), validate it at startup with a schema so a missing value fails fast, and keep secrets in a secrets manager or the platform's secret store, never in the repo. Use .env files only locally, and gitignore them.`,hook:`🔑 Code is public knowledge; keys live in the safe, and the app checks it has every key before it opens.`,tags:[`config`,`security`]},{id:`nest-why`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nestjs`,title:`What is NestJS, and why use it over plain Express?`,text:`NestJS is a TypeScript framework on top of Express (or Fastify) that adds structure: modules, dependency injection, decorators, and a consistent request pipeline (guards, pipes, interceptors, filters). Express gives you freedom; Nest gives large teams one agreed way to organise a backend, much like Angular does for frontends.`,hook:`🏛️ Express is a plot of land; Nest is the same land with a building plan already approved.`,tags:[`nestjs`,`architecture`]},{id:`nest-building-blocks`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nestjs`,title:`Explain modules, controllers and providers.`,text:`A module groups related features and declares what it imports and exports. Controllers handle HTTP routes and stay thin. Providers (services, repositories) hold the business logic and are injected where they're needed. A feature is typically one module with its controller and services.`,hook:`🏢 Module = department, controller = reception desk, provider = the specialist who does the actual work.`,tags:[`nestjs`,`modules`]},{id:`nest-di`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nestjs`,title:`How does dependency injection work in Nest, and why does it matter?`,text:`You declare dependencies in a class's constructor, and Nest's IoC container creates and supplies them (singletons by default). Classes don't build their own dependencies, so you can swap a real implementation for a mock in tests, or a different implementation per environment, without changing the class.`,hook:`🔌 Classes ask for a plug socket; the container decides what's plugged in, the real thing or a test double.`,tags:[`nestjs`,`dependency-injection`,`testing`]},{id:`nest-request-pipeline`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nestjs`,title:`Guards vs pipes vs interceptors vs exception filters?`,text:`A request flows: middleware → guards (can this request proceed? auth and roles) → interceptors (before) → pipes (validate and transform params/body) → handler → interceptors (after: shape the response, logging, caching) → exception filters (turn thrown errors into HTTP responses).`,hook:`🛫 Guard checks your ticket, pipe weighs your luggage, interceptor wraps the trip both ways, filter handles any crash.`,tags:[`nestjs`,`middleware`,`pipeline`]},{id:`nest-validation`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nestjs`,title:`How do you validate request bodies in Nest?`,text:`Define a DTO class with class-validator decorators and enable a global ValidationPipe with whitelist: true (strip unknown fields) and transform: true (convert to DTO instances and types). Invalid input gets a 400 before it reaches your handler.`,hook:`📐 The DTO is the shape of the door; anything that doesn't fit stays outside.`,tags:[`nestjs`,`validation`,`dto`]},{id:`nest-auth`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nestjs`,title:`How do you implement JWT auth and roles in Nest?`,text:`Use @nestjs/passport with a JWT strategy that verifies the token and attaches the user to the request, an AuthGuard('jwt') on protected routes, and a custom RolesGuard that reads a @Roles() decorator's metadata via Reflector. Keep access tokens short-lived and rotate refresh tokens.`,hook:`🎫 The JWT guard checks the ticket is real; the roles guard checks it's for the right section.`,tags:[`nestjs`,`auth`,`jwt`]},{id:`nest-testing`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nestjs`,title:`How do you test a NestJS service?`,text:`Use Test.createTestingModule to build a small module with the service under test and mock providers for its dependencies, then call the service directly. For end-to-end tests, boot the app and hit it with supertest, ideally against a real test database in a container.`,hook:`🧪 DI pays off in tests: swap the real parts for mocks without touching the class.`,tags:[`nestjs`,`testing`]},{id:`nest-architecture`,domain:`backend`,topic:`nestjs`,title:`How do you structure a growing Nest codebase?`,text:`One module per business feature (users, orders, billing) with clear exports, thin controllers, services for business rules, and repositories for data access. Put cross-cutting concerns (config, logging, auth) in shared modules, and avoid circular module dependencies, which usually mean a boundary is in the wrong place.`,hook:`🧱 Features as rooms with doors (exports), not open-plan where everyone reaches into everyone's drawers.`,tags:[`nestjs`,`architecture`]},{id:`php-request-model`,domain:`backend`,topic:`php-laravel`,title:`How does PHP's request model differ from Node's?`,text:`Classic PHP (PHP-FPM) is shared-nothing: each request runs in a fresh process state, and everything is torn down at the end. Nothing leaks between requests, but nothing is kept in memory either, so state goes in the database, cache or session store. Node is one long-running process that serves many requests and keeps in-memory state between them. Laravel Octane (Swoole/RoadRunner) keeps the app booted between requests for speed, which brings back Node-style concerns about leaked state.`,hook:`🧽 PHP wipes the whiteboard after every request; Node keeps writing on the same board all day.`,tags:[`php`,`runtime`]},{id:`laravel-mvc`,domain:`backend`,topic:`php-laravel`,title:`What does Laravel's MVC structure look like?`,text:`Routes (routes/web.php, routes/api.php) map URLs to controller methods. Controllers handle the request, use Eloquent models for data, and return a Blade view or JSON. Middleware wraps routes (auth, throttling), and form requests validate input.`,hook:`🧭 Route points the way, controller makes decisions, model talks to the database, view shows the result.`,tags:[`laravel`,`architecture`]},{id:`laravel-eloquent`,domain:`backend`,topic:`php-laravel`,title:`What is Eloquent, and how do you avoid N+1 queries with it?`,text:`Eloquent is Laravel's ActiveRecord ORM: each model maps to a table, and relationships are methods (hasMany, belongsTo). Accessing a relation in a loop lazily fires one query per row. Eager-load with with(), and turn on Model::preventLazyLoading() in development to catch it.`,hook:`🚌 with('author') sends one bus for all the authors instead of a taxi per post.`,tags:[`laravel`,`orm`,`performance`]},{id:`laravel-validation`,domain:`backend`,topic:`php-laravel`,title:`How does validation work in Laravel?`,text:`Call $request-validate([...]) or use a dedicated Form Request class with rules() and authorize(). Failed validation automatically redirects back with errors (web) or returns a 422 JSON response (API).`,hook:`🛂 Rules at the door: bad input never reaches the controller.`,tags:[`laravel`,`validation`]},{id:`laravel-migrations-queues`,domain:`backend`,topic:`php-laravel`,title:`What are migrations, queues and jobs in Laravel?`,text:`Migrations are versioned schema changes in PHP that you run with php artisan migrate. Jobs are classes for slow work (emails, image processing, webhooks) dispatched onto a queue (Redis, database, SQS) and processed by workers, so the HTTP response stays fast. Failed jobs can retry with backoff.`,hook:`📮 Don't make the customer wait at the counter while you post their parcel; take it, give a receipt, post it in the back.`,tags:[`laravel`,`queues`,`databases`]},{id:`laravel-auth`,domain:`backend`,topic:`php-laravel`,title:`How do you do API auth in Laravel?`,text:`Sanctum for SPA cookie-based auth (with CSRF protection) and simple personal API tokens. Passport when you need a full OAuth2 server. Authorise actions with Policies and Gates rather than scattered if checks.`,hook:`🎟️ Sanctum for your own apps, Passport when you're the identity provider for others.`,tags:[`laravel`,`auth`]},{id:`laravel-nextjs`,domain:`backend`,topic:`php-laravel`,title:`How would you connect a Next.js frontend to a Laravel API?`,text:`Expose JSON endpoints (API resources) from Laravel, authenticate with Sanctum (cookie auth on the same top-level domain, or bearer tokens), configure CORS for the frontend origin, and type the responses on the Next.js side, ideally with a schema or generated types. Server Components can call the API directly; client components go through Route Handlers or the same API.`,hook:`🔌 Laravel owns the data and rules; Next.js owns the screens; a typed JSON contract sits between them.`,tags:[`laravel`,`nextjs`,`full-stack`]},{id:`pg-indexes`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`How does an index speed up a query, and what does it cost?`,text:`A B-tree index keeps a column's values sorted with pointers to the rows, so lookups, range scans and sorts avoid reading the whole table. The costs: extra disk space, and slower writes, because every insert/update must update every index. Index the columns you filter, join and sort on, and check the plan to confirm it's used. In a composite index, column order matters: it helps queries that filter on its leading columns.`,hook:`📖 An index is the back-of-book index: instant lookups, but every new page means updating it.`,tags:[`indexes`,`performance`]},{id:`pg-explain`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`How do you find out why a query is slow?`,text:`Run EXPLAIN ANALYZE to see the actual plan, row counts and timings. Look for sequential scans on big tables, estimated vs actual rows that differ wildly (stale statistics: run ANALYZE), nested loops over many rows, and sorts spilling to disk. pgstatstatements shows which queries cost the most overall.`,hook:`🩻 EXPLAIN ANALYZE is an X-ray of the query: see the broken bone before you operate.`,tags:[`performance`,`explain`]},{id:`pg-joins`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`INNER vs LEFT vs FULL JOIN?`,text:`INNER JOIN returns only rows that match on both sides. LEFT JOIN returns every row from the left table, with NULLs where the right side has no match. FULL JOIN returns all rows from both sides, matched where possible.`,hook:`🤝 Inner = only couples, left = everyone from the left side plus partners if they have one, full = everyone at the party.`,tags:[`sql`,`joins`]},{id:`pg-acid`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`What do ACID and transactions guarantee?`,text:`Atomicity: all of a transaction's changes happen or none do. Consistency: constraints hold before and after. Isolation: concurrent transactions don't see each other's half-done work. Durability: once committed, it survives a crash. Wrap multi-step changes (debit one account, credit another) in a transaction.`,hook:`🏦 A bank transfer either fully happens or never did, even if the power goes out halfway.`,tags:[`transactions`,`consistency`]},{id:`pg-isolation`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`What are isolation levels, and which anomalies do they prevent?`,text:`Postgres defaults to Read Committed: each statement sees data committed before it started, so two reads in one transaction can differ. Repeatable Read gives the whole transaction one snapshot. Serializable makes concurrent transactions behave as if they ran one at a time, but may abort with serialization errors you must retry. For a "read then update" race, use SELECT … FOR UPDATE or an atomic UPDATE … SET x = x - 1.`,hook:`📸 Read Committed takes a new photo per statement; Repeatable Read keeps one photo for the whole transaction.`,tags:[`transactions`,`concurrency`]},{id:`pg-n-plus-one-orm`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`How do you avoid the N+1 query problem with an ORM?`,text:`Loading a list and then lazily loading a relation per item fires one query per row. Fix it with eager loading (a JOIN or include/relations), batching with WHERE id IN (…), or DataLoader in GraphQL. Log queries in development so you notice the pattern.`,hook:`🚌 One bus for all the passengers, not a taxi each.`,tags:[`orm`,`performance`]},{id:`pg-normalisation`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`Normalisation vs denormalisation?`,text:`Normalising stores each fact once (users, orders and products in separate tables linked by keys), which keeps data consistent and writes simple. Denormalising duplicates data (e.g. a total column or an author name on posts) to make reads faster, at the cost of keeping copies in sync. Start normalised and denormalise deliberately for proven hot paths.`,hook:`📇 Normalise to tell the truth once; denormalise to answer faster, and accept keeping the copies honest.`,tags:[`data-modelling`]},{id:`pg-jsonb`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`When would you use JSONB in Postgres?`,text:`For genuinely flexible or sparse attributes (settings, third-party payloads, per-tenant custom fields) inside an otherwise relational model. JSONB can be indexed with GIN and queried with operators. Don't use it to avoid designing a schema: fields you filter, join or constrain on regularly belong in real columns.`,hook:`👜 JSONB is a handbag for odds and ends, not a replacement for the wardrobe.`,tags:[`jsonb`,`data-modelling`]},{id:`pg-pagination`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`What are pagination options, and why is OFFSET slow?`,text:`LIMIT/OFFSET is simple, but the database still reads and discards all the skipped rows, so deep pages get slow, and rows shift when data changes between requests. Keyset (cursor) pagination uses the last seen value (WHERE createdat < $last ORDER BY createdat DESC LIMIT 20), which stays fast and stable with an index.`,hook:`📚 OFFSET counts every book from the start of the shelf; a cursor keeps a bookmark.`,tags:[`pagination`,`performance`]},{id:`pg-connection-pooling`,domain:`databases`,topic:`postgresql`,title:`What is connection pooling, and why does it matter?`,text:`Opening a Postgres connection is expensive, and each one uses server memory, so the database supports only a limited number. A pool (in the app, or PgBouncer in front) reuses a small set of connections across many requests. Serverless functions especially need one, or they exhaust the database's connection limit under load.`,hook:`🚕 A taxi rank: reuse the waiting cars instead of buying a new car for every trip.`,tags:[`connections`,`scaling`]},{id:`git-everyday`,domain:`devops`,topic:`git`,title:`What do the everyday Git commands do?`,text:`git add stages changes for the next commit. git commit records the staged snapshot with a message. git push sends local commits to the remote. git pull fetches remote changes and merges (or rebases) them into your branch. git switch -c creates and switches to a new branch so work stays isolated from main.`,hook:`📸 Add = pose for the photo, commit = take it, push = post it, pull = see everyone else's.`,tags:[`git`,`basics`]},{id:`git-merge-vs-rebase`,domain:`devops`,topic:`git`,title:`Merge vs rebase: when do you use each?`,text:`Merge keeps the true history with a merge commit and never rewrites anything. Rebase replays your commits on top of the latest main for a linear history, but it rewrites commit hashes, which is dangerous on a branch others have pulled. Rule of thumb: rebase your own branch to tidy it before a PR; merge once it's shared.`,hook:`🚂 Merge couples two trains together; rebase moves your carriages to the front of the new train, so don't do it while passengers are aboard.`,tags:[`git`,`history`]},{id:`git-merge-conflicts`,domain:`devops`,topic:`git`,title:`How do you resolve a merge conflict?`,text:`Pull or rebase onto main, open each conflicting file, and decide with the other author's intent in mind (ask them if it's unclear). Remove the markers, run the tests, then commit. Have one real example ready to describe.`,hook:`🤝 A conflict is two people editing the same sentence: understand both versions before you pick the words.`,tags:[`git`,`collaboration`]},{id:`git-pull-requests`,domain:`devops`,topic:`git`,title:`Why use pull requests instead of pushing to main?`,text:`A PR is a checkpoint where the team reviews the diff before it lands, catching bugs or better approaches early. It records why a change was made, and it's usually where CI runs before merge is allowed.`,hook:`🧑‍⚖️ A PR is a second pair of eyes and a paper trail, before it's permanent.`,tags:[`git`,`code-review`]},{id:`git-workflow`,domain:`devops`,topic:`git`,title:`Walk through your branching and PR workflow.`,text:`Short-lived feature branch off main, small focused commits with clear messages, a PR with context and screenshots once it's testable, CI green, address review comments, then squash-merge or rebase-merge according to team convention.`,hook:`🌿 Branch small, commit often, explain the why, merge green.`,tags:[`git`,`workflow`]},{id:`docker-container-vs-vm`,domain:`devops`,topic:`docker`,title:`What is a container, and how is it different from a VM?`,text:`A container packages an app with its dependencies, runtime and config so it behaves the same everywhere. A VM virtualises a whole operating system with its own kernel; a container shares the host kernel and isolates only the app layer, so it starts in seconds and is far lighter. An image is the packaged template; a container is a running instance of it.`,hook:`🏢 VMs are separate houses with their own foundations; containers are flats sharing one building's foundations.`,tags:[`docker`,`containers`]},{id:`docker-multi-stage`,domain:`devops`,topic:`docker`,title:`What is a multi-stage build, and why bother?`,text:`A Dockerfile with several FROM stages: one stage installs dependencies and builds with the full toolchain, and a final stage copies only the built output into a minimal runtime image. The production image is much smaller and doesn't ship dev dependencies or build tools, which helps deploy speed and attack surface.`,hook:`🏗️ Build in the workshop, ship only the finished furniture.`,tags:[`docker`,`images`,`security`]},{id:`docker-layer-caching`,domain:`devops`,topic:`docker`,title:`How does Docker layer caching work?`,text:`Each instruction creates a cached layer, and a change invalidates that layer and everything after it. Order instructions from least to most frequently changed: copy package.json and install before copying the source, so a code change doesn't re-run npm install.`,hook:`🧁 Layers are a cake: change the bottom tier and you rebake everything above it.`,tags:[`docker`,`caching`,`ci`]},{id:`ci-what`,domain:`devops`,topic:`ci-cd`,title:`What does CI/CD actually mean?`,text:`Continuous Integration: every push is automatically built and tested, so integration problems surface immediately instead of piling up. Continuous Delivery packages passing code ready to ship, with a manual approval to deploy; Continuous Deployment goes all the way to production automatically. Both shrink the gap between writing code and users running it.`,hook:`🏭 CI checks every part as it comes off the line; CD ships the boxes the moment they pass inspection.`,tags:[`ci`,`cd`]},{id:`ci-stage-order`,domain:`devops`,topic:`ci-cd`,title:`What's a sensible order of stages in a CI pipeline?`,text:`Install (with a cache), then lint and typecheck, then unit tests, then build, then integration/E2E tests against a preview, then deploy. Cheapest checks first, so a broken PR fails in seconds rather than after a ten-minute build.`,hook:`⚡ Fail fast: run the 5-second check before the 5-minute one.`,tags:[`ci`,`pipelines`]},{id:`sd-approach`,domain:`system-design`,topic:`fundamentals`,title:`How do you approach a system design interview?`,text:`1) Clarify requirements, both functional and non-functional (scale, latency, consistency). 2) Rough estimates (requests per second, storage). 3) Sketch the high-level design: clients, API, services, data stores. 4) Define the APIs and data model. 5) Deep-dive the hardest part. 6) Discuss bottlenecks, failure modes and trade-offs. Think aloud and confirm assumptions as you go.`,hook:`🗺️ Ask, estimate, sketch, detail, stress-test, and narrate the whole way.`,tags:[`approach`,`interview`]},{id:`sd-scaling`,domain:`system-design`,topic:`fundamentals`,title:`Vertical vs horizontal scaling?`,text:`Vertical means a bigger machine: simple, but there's a ceiling and a single point of failure. Horizontal means more machines behind a load balancer: near-unlimited and resilient, but it requires stateless services and shared stores for sessions, files and caches.`,hook:`🏋️ Vertical = one stronger person; horizontal = more people, who must not keep notes in their own pockets.`,tags:[`scaling`]},{id:`sd-load-balancer`,domain:`system-design`,topic:`fundamentals`,title:`What does a load balancer do?`,text:`It spreads traffic across healthy instances (round-robin, least connections, hashing), removes failing ones using health checks, and often terminates TLS. L4 balancers route by IP and port; L7 balancers understand HTTP and can route by path or header.`,hook:`🚦 A traffic officer who sends each car to the least busy lane and closes lanes that have crashed.`,tags:[`load-balancing`]},{id:`sd-caching`,domain:`system-design`,topic:`fundamentals`,title:`Where and how do you cache?`,text:`Caches sit at several layers: browser, CDN (static and edge-cacheable responses), app-level (Redis/Memcached for hot reads or expensive computations) and the database's own buffer. The common pattern is cache-aside: read the cache, and on a miss load from the DB and populate it. The hard parts are invalidation, TTLs, and stampedes when a hot key expires.`,hook:`🧊 Keep the drinks you serve most in the fridge by the bar, and know when to throw out the stale ones.`,tags:[`caching`,`redis`,`cdn`]},{id:`sd-cap`,domain:`system-design`,topic:`fundamentals`,title:`What is the CAP theorem?`,text:`When a network partition happens, a distributed system must choose between Consistency (every read sees the latest write, or errors) and Availability (every request gets a response, possibly stale). Partitions can't be ruled out, so the real choice is CP or AP per feature: payments lean CP, while social feeds and likes lean AP with eventual consistency.`,hook:`✂️ When the line between data centres is cut, you either stop answering or risk answering with old news.`,tags:[`distributed-systems`,`consistency`]},{id:`sd-queues`,domain:`system-design`,topic:`fundamentals`,title:`When would you use a message queue?`,text:`To decouple producers from consumers and absorb spikes: slow work (emails, video processing), fan-out to several services, and retries without blocking the request. Queues (SQS, RabbitMQ) distribute tasks to workers; logs/streams (Kafka) keep an ordered, replayable history for many consumers. Design consumers to be idempotent, because messages can arrive more than once.`,hook:`📬 A queue is an inbox: the sender doesn't wait for you to read it, and you might read the same letter twice.`,tags:[`queues`,`async`,`kafka`]},{id:`sd-sql-vs-nosql`,domain:`system-design`,topic:`fundamentals`,title:`SQL vs NoSQL: how do you choose?`,text:`Choose SQL (Postgres, MySQL) for relational data, transactions, joins and flexible querying, which covers most apps. Choose NoSQL for specific access patterns at very large scale: key-value (Redis, DynamoDB) for fast lookups, document stores (MongoDB) for nested aggregates, wide-column (Cassandra) for huge write volumes, graph databases for relationships. Start with SQL unless a known access pattern demands otherwise.`,hook:`🧰 SQL is the general-purpose workshop; NoSQL stores are specialist tools you pick for one job done at huge scale.`,tags:[`databases`,`data-modelling`]},{id:`sd-reliability`,domain:`system-design`,topic:`fundamentals`,title:`How do you design for reliability?`,text:`Remove single points of failure (replicas, multiple zones), set timeouts on every network call, retry with exponential backoff and jitter only for idempotent operations, add circuit breakers so a failing dependency doesn't take you down, degrade gracefully (serve cached or partial results), and monitor golden signals (latency, traffic, errors, saturation) with alerts.`,hook:`🪂 Assume every call can fail: set a timeout, retry politely, cut the cord if it keeps failing, and keep flying with what you have.`,tags:[`reliability`,`resilience`]},{id:`sd-url-shortener`,domain:`system-design`,topic:`fundamentals`,title:`Design a URL shortener (outline).`,text:`Requirements: create a short code for a long URL, redirect fast, and (optionally) count analytics. API: POST /links, then GET /:code returns a 301/302. Generate codes with base62 of a unique id (or random plus a collision check), and store code → url in a key-value store or indexed SQL table. Reads vastly outnumber writes, so cache hot codes and serve redirects from the edge; record analytics asynchronously through a queue.`,hook:`🔗 Tiny write, giant read: optimise everything for the redirect.`,tags:[`design-exercise`]},{id:`sec-xss-react`,domain:`security`,topic:`web-security`,title:`Where does XSS come from in a React app?`,text:`React escapes anything rendered through {value}, so the classic script injection is mostly closed. It reopens with dangerouslySetInnerHTML (sanitise with DOMPurify), user-controlled URLs in href/src (a javascript: URL), and third-party scripts. A Content Security Policy adds defence in depth.`,hook:`🛡️ React locks the front door; dangerouslySetInnerHTML and javascript: links are windows you opened yourself.`,tags:[`xss`,`react`]},{id:`sec-csrf`,domain:`security`,topic:`web-security`,title:`What is CSRF, and how do you prevent it?`,text:`A malicious site makes the user's browser send a request to your site, and the browser automatically attaches the user's cookies. Mitigate with SameSite cookies, CSRF tokens on state-changing requests, and checking the Origin header. Bearer tokens sent in an Authorization header aren't vulnerable, because the browser doesn't attach them automatically.`,hook:`🎣 CSRF tricks your browser into using your own badge on someone else's errand.`,tags:[`csrf`,`cookies`]},{id:`sec-jwt-storage`,domain:`security`,topic:`web-security`,title:`JWT in an httpOnly cookie vs localStorage?`,text:`localStorage is readable by any script on the page, so a single XSS bug lets an attacker steal the token. An HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie can't be read by JavaScript, which closes that theft route, but cookies are sent automatically, so you take on CSRF care instead. Use short-lived access tokens with refresh tokens either way.`,hook:`🍪 localStorage is a key under the doormat (XSS finds it); an httpOnly cookie is a key in a safe that opens itself for anyone who rings (so check who's ringing: CSRF).`,tags:[`jwt`,`auth`,`cookies`]},{id:`sec-validation`,domain:`security`,topic:`web-security`,title:`Why validate on both client and server?`,text:`Client-side validation is for user experience: instant feedback without a round trip. It isn't security, because anyone can skip the frontend and call the API directly with curl or Postman. Server-side validation is the real security boundary.`,hook:`🚧 The frontend is a polite sign; the backend is the actual gate.`,tags:[`validation`]},{id:`sec-csp`,domain:`security`,topic:`web-security`,title:`What does a Content Security Policy restrict, and what breaks under one?`,text:`CSP is a header (or meta tag) that allowlists where scripts, styles, images, frames and connections may come from, and blocks inline scripts and eval unless they carry a nonce or hash. What commonly breaks: inline event handlers (onclick="…"), inline <script blocks, and third-party widgets that inject their own scripts.`,hook:`📋 CSP is a guest list for code: if a script's name isn't on it, it doesn't get in.`,tags:[`csp`,`headers`]},{id:`sec-sql-injection`,domain:`security`,topic:`web-security`,title:`What is SQL injection, and why should a frontend dev care?`,text:`User input gets concatenated into a SQL string, so crafted input changes the query's meaning, e.g. turning a login check into one that's always true. The fix is parameterised queries. It's the same lesson as XSS: never trust input, whether it's going to a database, into HTML or into a URL.`,hook:`💉 Concatenating input into a query lets the user finish your sentence for you.`,tags:[`injection`,`databases`]},{id:`sec-password-hashing`,domain:`security`,topic:`web-security`,title:`Why hash passwords instead of storing them?`,text:`A hash is one-way: you can check a password against it but can't reverse it. If the database leaks, hashes aren't directly usable, whereas plaintext hands over every password. Password hashes (bcrypt, Argon2) are deliberately slow and salted, which makes brute-forcing at scale impractical.`,hook:`🥚 You can scramble an egg, but you can't unscramble it, and a slow scramble makes guessing very expensive.`,tags:[`auth`,`hashing`]},{id:`auth-oauth-vs-oidc`,domain:`security`,topic:`oauth-owasp`,title:`OAuth 2.0 vs OpenID Connect?`,text:`OAuth 2.0 is about authorisation: letting an app access an API on a user's behalf with an access token, without the user's password. OpenID Connect is a layer on top for authentication: it adds an ID token (a signed JWT) that says who the user is. "Sign in with Google" is OIDC; "let this app read my Google Drive" is OAuth.`,hook:`🪪 OAuth hands over a valet key (what you may do); OIDC adds an ID card (who you are).`,tags:[`oauth`,`oidc`]},{id:`auth-pkce`,domain:`security`,topic:`oauth-owasp`,title:`Walk through the Authorization Code flow with PKCE.`,text:`The app makes a random codeverifier and sends its hash (codechallenge) when redirecting the user to the provider. The user logs in and consents; the provider redirects back with a short-lived code. The app exchanges the code plus the original verifier for tokens. A stolen code is useless without the verifier, which is why PKCE is the standard for SPAs and mobile apps (the old implicit flow is deprecated).`,hook:`🎟️ You show half a torn ticket on the way in and must produce the matching half to collect your coat.`,tags:[`oauth`,`pkce`,`spa`]},{id:`auth-access-refresh`,domain:`security`,topic:`oauth-owasp`,title:`Access tokens vs refresh tokens?`,text:`Access tokens are short-lived (minutes) and sent with each API call, so a leaked one expires quickly. Refresh tokens are long-lived, kept more securely (an httpOnly cookie or the server side), and only used to get new access tokens. Rotate refresh tokens on every use and revoke the whole family if an old one is reused, which signals theft.`,hook:`🎫 The access token is a day pass; the refresh token is the membership card you keep at home.`,tags:[`tokens`,`jwt`]},{id:`auth-jwt-validation`,domain:`security`,topic:`oauth-owasp`,title:`How do you validate a JWT?`,text:`Verify the signature with the expected algorithm and key (never accept alg: none, and don't let the token choose the algorithm), then check exp, nbf, iss and aud. Remember the payload is only base64-encoded, not encrypted, so never put secrets in it.`,hook:`🔏 Check the seal, the expiry date, who issued it and who it's addressed to, and remember anyone can read the letter.`,tags:[`jwt`,`security`]},{id:`owasp-top10`,domain:`security`,topic:`oauth-owasp`,title:`What is the OWASP Top 10?`,text:`A regularly updated list of the most critical web app risks. The recent list includes Broken Access Control, Cryptographic Failures, Injection (SQL, NoSQL, command, XSS), Insecure Design, Security Misconfiguration, Vulnerable and Outdated Components, Identification and Authentication Failures, Software and Data Integrity Failures, Security Logging and Monitoring Failures, and Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF). Interviewers care less about reciting the list than about a concrete defence for two or three items you've actually handled.`,hook:`🔟 Who can do what, secrets, injection, design, config, dependencies, login, integrity, logging, SSRF.`,tags:[`owasp`,`security`]},{id:`owasp-access-control`,domain:`security`,topic:`oauth-owasp`,title:`What is broken access control, and how do you prevent it?`,text:`Users can act outside their permissions, most often IDOR: changing /orders/123 to /orders/124 returns someone else's order because the server only checked that the user was logged in, not that the order is theirs. Prevent it by checking ownership and role on the server for every request, denying by default, and centralising the checks.`,hook:`🔑 Being inside the hotel doesn't mean your key opens every room.`,tags:[`owasp`,`authorization`,`idor`]},{id:`owasp-ssrf`,domain:`security`,topic:`oauth-owasp`,title:`What is SSRF?`,text:`Server-Side Request Forgery: the server fetches a URL the attacker controls (e.g. "import an image from this URL") and gets tricked into calling internal services or cloud metadata endpoints such as 169.254.169.254. Defend with allowlists of hosts, blocking private IP ranges after DNS resolution, disabling redirects, and network-level egress rules.`,hook:`📞 SSRF tricks your server into making calls from inside the building where the doors are unlocked.`,tags:[`owasp`,`ssrf`]},{id:`auth-rate-limiting`,domain:`security`,topic:`oauth-owasp`,title:`How should you rate-limit and protect login endpoints?`,text:`Rate-limit by IP and by account, use exponential backoff or temporary lockouts, return the same generic error for "no such user" and "wrong password" to avoid user enumeration, add MFA, and log and alert on spikes. Store rate-limit counters in a shared store like Redis when you run several instances.`,hook:`🚪 Slow the door down after a few wrong keys, and never say which part of the key was wrong.`,tags:[`rate-limiting`,`brute-force`]},{id:`ai-workflow`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`ai-assisted-development`,title:`How do you use AI tools in your workflow?`,text:`Spec first, then generate against it, so the output has a target to be checked against. Give the tool the real files, conventions and constraints. Review every diff like a junior's PR. Use skills/commands and tool connectors (MCP) for repeatable workflows. Use it for scaffolding, unfamiliar errors and exploring a new codebase, and say concretely what you did, not "I let it write my features".`,hook:`🧭 You're the pilot with a very fast co-pilot: you set the destination and you land the plane.`,tags:[`ai`,`workflow`]},{id:`ai-review`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`ai-assisted-development`,title:`Why review AI-generated code instead of accepting it?`,text:`It can be confidently wrong: plausible code that misses an edge case, uses a deprecated API, or does something slightly different from what you asked. You own whatever ships, so verify against reality (run it, test it, check edge cases), not against the AI's own description of what it did.`,hook:`🧐 Treat AI output like a confident intern's PR: probably right, never unread.`,tags:[`ai`,`code-review`]},{id:`ai-context`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`ai-assisted-development`,title:`Why does more context produce better AI output?`,text:`The model only knows what's in its context, not your codebase's conventions or the reasons behind past decisions. Pointing it at the relevant files and stating constraints explicitly (library versions, naming, performance limits) narrows the output to something that fits, instead of generic code that technically works.`,hook:`🗺️ A great driver with no map still takes the wrong turn; give it the map.`,tags:[`ai`,`prompting`]},{id:`ai-risks`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`ai-assisted-development`,title:`What are the risks of AI-assisted development?`,text:`Confident wrong answers, outdated APIs, subtle security holes, code nobody on the team understands, licence questions, and leaking secrets or customer data into prompts. Mitigate with review, tests, secret scanning, clear rules about what can be pasted where, and keeping humans accountable for every merge.`,hook:`⚠️ Fast, fluent and occasionally wrong: speed is the gain, verification is the price.`,tags:[`ai`,`security`]},{id:`rag-what`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`rag-llm`,title:`What is RAG, and why use it instead of fine-tuning?`,text:`Retrieval-Augmented Generation fetches relevant documents at query time and puts them in the prompt, so the model answers from your current data and can cite sources. It's cheaper and fresher than fine-tuning, and data updates are instant. Fine-tuning changes the model's behaviour or style; it isn't a good way to teach it facts that change.`,hook:`📚 RAG is an open-book exam: the model looks things up instead of trying to memorise the textbook.`,tags:[`rag`,`llm`]},{id:`rag-pipeline`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`rag-llm`,title:`Walk through a RAG pipeline.`,text:`Ingest: split documents into chunks, embed each chunk into a vector, and store the vectors with metadata. Query: embed the question, retrieve the top-k similar chunks (often hybrid vector + keyword search, then a reranker), build a prompt with those chunks and instructions to answer only from them, generate, and return the answer with citations.`,hook:`✂️🔢🗄️🔍✍️ Chop, embed, store, retrieve, answer with receipts.`,tags:[`rag`,`embeddings`,`architecture`]},{id:`rag-embeddings`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`rag-llm`,title:`What are embeddings?`,text:`An embedding is a list of numbers that represents the meaning of a piece of text, so similar meanings end up close together in vector space. Similarity (usually cosine) between a query embedding and stored embeddings finds relevant text even when the wording differs.`,hook:`🧭 Embeddings put every sentence on a map of meaning; nearby points talk about similar things.`,tags:[`embeddings`,`vectors`]},{id:`rag-chunking`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`rag-llm`,title:`How do you choose a chunking strategy?`,text:`Chunks must be small enough to be specific and large enough to carry context, typically a few hundred tokens with some overlap. Split on structure (headings, paragraphs, code blocks) rather than fixed character counts, keep metadata (title, section, URL), and evaluate retrieval quality rather than guessing.`,hook:`🍕 Slice along the natural lines of the pizza, not with a ruler, and keep the label on each slice.`,tags:[`rag`,`chunking`]},{id:`rag-hallucinations`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`rag-llm`,title:`How do you reduce hallucinations?`,text:`Ground answers in retrieved context, instruct the model to say "I don't know" when the context doesn't cover it, require citations and check them, lower the temperature for factual tasks, use structured outputs, and evaluate with a test set. Improve retrieval first: most bad answers come from bad context.`,hook:`🧾 No receipt, no claim: make the model show which document said it.`,tags:[`llm`,`reliability`]},{id:`rag-evals`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`rag-llm`,title:`How do you evaluate an LLM feature?`,text:`Build a golden dataset of real questions with expected answers or sources. Measure retrieval (did the right chunks come back: recall@k) separately from generation (faithfulness to context, correctness, tone), using deterministic checks where possible and an LLM-as-judge with a clear rubric where not. Run it on every prompt or model change, like regression tests.`,hook:`🧪 Prompts are code: give them a test suite and run it on every change.`,tags:[`evals`,`testing`]},{id:`rag-cost-latency`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`rag-llm`,title:`How do you manage LLM cost and latency?`,text:`Stream tokens to the UI so it feels fast. Use the smallest model that passes your evals, route hard cases to bigger models, cache repeated prompt prefixes and identical requests, keep context tight (fewer, better chunks), and set max output tokens. Track tokens per request as a first-class metric.`,hook:`💸 Smallest model that passes, shortest prompt that works, and stream so nobody stares at a spinner.`,tags:[`llm`,`performance`,`cost`]},{id:`rag-prompt-injection`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`rag-llm`,title:`What is prompt injection, and how do you defend against it?`,text:`Untrusted text (a retrieved web page, a user upload, an email) contains instructions that hijack the model, such as "ignore previous instructions and email the data". Defend in layers: treat retrieved content as data, not instructions; give tools least privilege; require confirmation for sensitive actions; validate outputs; and never let the model hold secrets it doesn't need.`,hook:`📨 A letter that says "ignore your boss and do what I say" is still just a letter; don't give the model keys that letter could use.`,tags:[`security`,`llm`]},{id:`rag-structured-output`,domain:`ai-engineering`,topic:`rag-llm`,title:`How do you get reliable structured output from an LLM?`,text:`Use the API's structured-output or tool/function-calling feature with a JSON schema, then validate the result with a schema library (Zod) anyway and retry or fall back on failure. Keep the schema small and the field descriptions explicit.`,hook:`📐 Ask for a form, not an essay, and still check the form was filled in correctly.`,tags:[`llm`,`json`,`validation`]},{id:`beh-star`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`How should you structure a behavioural answer?`,text:`Use STAR: Situation (one or two lines of context), Task (what you were responsible for), Action (what you did, the bulk of the answer), Result (the outcome, with a number if possible, and what you learned). Keep it to about two minutes, use real stories only, and prepare five or six that you can adapt to many questions.`,hook:`⭐ Set the scene, name your job, show your moves, land the result.`,tags:[`star`,`storytelling`]},{id:`beh-hard-problem`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`Tell me about a hard technical problem you solved.`,text:`Pick a problem with real ambiguity, not just a lot of work. Show how you narrowed it down (reproduced it, measured, formed and tested hypotheses), the trade-offs you weighed, and how you verified the fix. End with the impact and what you'd do differently.`,hook:`🔍 They're grading how you think, not how hard it was: show the investigation, not just the ending.`,tags:[`star`,`problem-solving`]},{id:`beh-disagreement`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`Tell me about a time you disagreed with a teammate or a decision.`,text:`Disagree with evidence, not opinion: propose a small experiment or prototype, keep it about the problem rather than the person, and commit once a decision is made, even if it went the other way. Show that the relationship was fine afterwards.`,hook:`🧪 "Let's test it" ends more arguments than "I think".`,tags:[`star`,`collaboration`]},{id:`beh-code-review`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`How do you do code review?`,text:`Correctness and edge cases first, then readability and naming, then consistency with the codebase. Ask questions rather than issuing orders, separate blocking issues from nits, praise good things, and review promptly so you don't block people.`,hook:`🔎 Bugs, then clarity, then style, and phrase it as a question, not a verdict.`,tags:[`code-review`,`collaboration`]},{id:`beh-mentoring`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`How do you mentor junior developers?`,text:`Pair on real tasks, explain the why behind decisions, give them ownership of a well-scoped piece, review generously, and widen the scope step by step as they grow. Measure success by what they can do without you.`,hook:`🌱 Hand them the wheel on a quiet road first, then a busier one.`,tags:[`leadership`,`mentoring`]},{id:`beh-deadlines`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`How do you handle a tight deadline?`,text:`Cut scope explicitly and early instead of quietly cutting quality. Protect the core flow, agree the trade-offs with stakeholders, and write down what was cut and why so it can come back later.`,hook:`✂️ Cut features in the open; never cut corners in secret.`,tags:[`prioritisation`,`delivery`]},{id:`beh-tech-debt`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`How do you approach technical debt?`,text:`Make it visible (log it with its impact), pay it down alongside feature work in the same area, and argue for it in terms the business cares about, such as delivery speed, incidents and onboarding time, rather than code beauty.`,hook:`💳 Tech debt is a credit card: fine to use, dangerous to ignore the statement.`,tags:[`tech-debt`,`prioritisation`]},{id:`beh-choosing-libraries`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`How do you choose a library?`,text:`First check whether the problem actually needs one. Then weigh bundle size, maintenance activity, types, ecosystem and community, security history, and how hard it would be to swap out later. Prefer boring, well-maintained options for core infrastructure.`,hook:`🛒 Do I need it, is it healthy, and can I leave if it goes bad?`,tags:[`decision-making`,`dependencies`]},{id:`beh-onboarding`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`How do you get up to speed on a new codebase?`,text:`Run it locally, trace one feature end to end, read the tests and recent PRs, ask people which parts hurt, and ship something small early to learn the deploy path.`,hook:`🧵 Pull one thread all the way through before trying to see the whole fabric.`,tags:[`onboarding`]},{id:`beh-production-bug`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`Tell me about a production bug you handled.`,text:`Reproduce it, contain it first (rollback, feature flag), then fix the root cause, add a test that would have caught it, and write a short blameless post-mortem with follow-ups. Pick a real one and give numbers (users affected, time to recovery).`,hook:`🚒 Put out the fire, find the cause, fireproof the building, write the report.`,tags:[`incidents`,`debugging`]},{id:`beh-frontend-architecture`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`How do you structure a large frontend app?`,text:`Feature-based folders, clear layering (pure logic → state → components), shared UI primitives, typed API boundaries, and one agreed way to do each thing (fetching, errors, styling, forms). Boundaries matter more than any particular folder name.`,hook:`🏙️ Neighbourhoods (features), zoning rules (layers) and one set of building codes (conventions).`,tags:[`architecture`]},{id:`beh-improve-last-project`,domain:`behavioural`,topic:`senior-behavioural`,title:`What would you improve in your last project?`,text:`Show self-awareness without trashing your own work: name one or two concrete things (test coverage on a risky area, an early architectural choice, monitoring), explain why they matter, and how you'd approach them now. It signals growth, not failure.`,hook:`🔭 Good engineers can always name their next fix.`,tags:[`reflection`]}];export{e as default};